WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.292
- He jumps up.

00:00:01.292 --> 00:00:05.213
"You want some? Like Deebo. You want some too?"

00:00:05.213 --> 00:00:07.590
I'm like, "No, I don't!"

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:16.683
- Welcome to "Untold Stories."

00:00:16.683 --> 00:00:18.768
I'm your host, Master Tesfatsion. We've had

00:00:18.768 --> 00:00:21.312
12 incredible episodes in Season 1, and

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:23.106
what better way to have a season finale

00:00:23.273 --> 00:00:25.817
than here in Miami, in South Beach during

00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:26.651
Super Bowl week? And

00:00:26.651 --> 00:00:28.319
I'm excited for my next guest.

00:00:28.653 --> 00:00:30.071
He is a New Orleans native.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:31.906
He is a Hurricane great, a national

00:00:31.906 --> 00:00:34.617
champion, a Ravens legend, a Super Bowl

00:00:34.617 --> 00:00:38.121
champion, a member of the NFL 100, a Hall

00:00:38.121 --> 00:00:39.748
of Famer and one of the greatest safeties

00:00:39.748 --> 00:00:40.415
of all time.

00:00:40.665 --> 00:00:42.042
I hope I'm doin' you justice with this

00:00:42.042 --> 00:00:43.501
introduction. Ladies and gentlemen,

00:00:43.501 --> 00:00:44.044
please give it up

00:00:44.419 --> 00:00:47.213
for Ed Reed.
[Applause]

00:00:48.089 --> 00:00:51.676
My man. What's up, baby? How you doin'?

00:00:51.676 --> 00:00:54.137
- I'm good, man. No complaints, man.
- How's retirement been treatin' you, man?

00:00:54.179 --> 00:00:56.097
- Retirement is semi.

00:00:56.139 --> 00:00:58.266
Um, football is a short-lived career,

00:00:58.391 --> 00:00:58.767
you know?

00:00:58.767 --> 00:01:02.228
So I had—you have to plan for, you know,

00:01:02.228 --> 00:01:03.104
the next chapter.

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:03.938
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:01:03.938 --> 00:01:06.191
So, yeah, I'm retired from football, but

00:01:06.191 --> 00:01:07.067
I'm still around it.

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:08.359
I'm still doin' a lotta things.

00:01:08.485 --> 00:01:09.486
- With all that planning you're doin', do

00:01:09.486 --> 00:01:09.944
you get time to,

00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:12.113
to play pool or nah?
- A little bit, man.

00:01:12.113 --> 00:01:14.532
I got a pool table in my house, so, you know—
- All right, cool.

00:01:14.532 --> 00:01:16.367
I shoot a lil' bit every, every now and then.

00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:18.369
But now it's the ping-pong, table tennis—

00:01:18.369 --> 00:01:18.953
- Oh, that's what you on?

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:19.412
- Yeah.

00:01:19.537 --> 00:01:20.997
- All right, before you grab that stick now, I got a,

00:01:21.039 --> 00:01:21.706
I got a gift for you, all right?

00:01:21.706 --> 00:01:22.874
Since you the OG,

00:01:23.166 --> 00:01:23.792
you're in a pool hall,

00:01:23.792 --> 00:01:24.793
I gotta make sure the OG feels

00:01:24.793 --> 00:01:25.210
comfortable.

00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:25.752
- Always.

00:01:25.794 --> 00:01:29.172
You know, I heard you're a big cigar aficionado.
- Yes, sir.

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:31.341
- So, Akshay's got some cigars for you here.

00:01:31.341 --> 00:01:31.925
- Whoa now.

00:01:31.925 --> 00:01:33.343
- Shout-out to Glenn, who told me this is one of your

00:01:33.343 --> 00:01:34.844
favorite brands right here.

00:01:34.844 --> 00:01:35.261
- Yeah, man. No doubt.

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:35.762
You know what?

00:01:35.804 --> 00:01:38.807
I actually got my cutter in my lighter.
- See?

00:01:38.807 --> 00:01:41.434
- So you take this right here—
- All we need is the ashtray.

00:01:41.434 --> 00:01:43.478
- Never leave home without it.
- Lemme hold this for you right here.

00:01:43.478 --> 00:01:44.771
- Yeah.
- When did you get into cigars?

00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:45.855
- Got some nice Plasencias here.

00:01:46.022 --> 00:01:47.899
I got into cigars in college, man.

00:01:48.024 --> 00:01:49.776
And, um, me and Bubba Franks—
- College?

00:01:49.776 --> 00:01:50.527
- Yeah, me and Bubba

00:01:50.527 --> 00:01:52.654
Franks used to go hit golf balls all the

00:01:52.654 --> 00:01:54.155
time, losin' golf balls.

00:01:54.489 --> 00:01:55.406
And, um,

00:01:56.908 --> 00:01:59.994
I was smoking Montecristo and, um,

00:02:00.203 --> 00:02:02.956
Arturo Fuente and Reggie Wayne called me

00:02:02.956 --> 00:02:04.374
and was like, "Man, I got some premium

00:02:04.374 --> 00:02:05.125
sticks for you.

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:06.042
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:02:06.042 --> 00:02:08.128
So I'mma hook you up." And sure enough,

00:02:08.128 --> 00:02:09.254
that's what he did, man.

00:02:09.254 --> 00:02:10.505
And it's been good since then.

00:02:10.672 --> 00:02:11.756
- All right, you can go ahead and get cut up real quick.

00:02:12.215 --> 00:02:13.341
- Yeah, so I got a dual cutter.

00:02:13.341 --> 00:02:15.051
I got the V and the straight cutter.

00:02:15.051 --> 00:02:15.635
- Is that what you do? Lemme see.

00:02:16.302 --> 00:02:17.846
- Yeah, so you got the V, straight.

00:02:18.555 --> 00:02:19.889
I'mma go with a V cut.

00:02:19.889 --> 00:02:21.349
Actually, I'mma crown cut it,

00:02:22.016 --> 00:02:22.934
which most people

00:02:23.685 --> 00:02:25.520
just, like, don't really know too much about.

00:02:27.397 --> 00:02:29.440
You know what I'm talkin' about? Crown for a king.
- Yeah.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:29.899
- You smokin' with me or

00:02:29.899 --> 00:02:30.567
I'm by myself?

00:02:30.567 --> 00:02:31.609
- Uh, you gonna smoke with you right here.

00:02:31.609 --> 00:02:32.652
I'm gonna smoke after to celebrate the

00:02:32.652 --> 00:02:34.904
season, all right?
- Say no more.

00:02:34.946 --> 00:02:36.197
- That sound good? Does that sound good?
- Say no more. More, the merrier.

00:02:36.197 --> 00:02:37.949
- While you doin' that, while you lightin' that,
go ahead and grab that stick.

00:02:37.949 --> 00:02:41.828
- I'll take two.
- Hopefully that ashtray

00:02:41.828 --> 00:02:43.997
don't fall down. And hopefully I get this

00:02:43.997 --> 00:02:44.455
break right.

00:02:46.082 --> 00:02:47.041
Oh, I scratched on the first one. Damn.

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:54.132
- You haven't been practicing much.
All right, lemme see what I got here.

00:02:54.132 --> 00:02:55.133
- Table open for you.

00:02:55.842 --> 00:02:58.678
- Shoot this one ball in the side pocket.

00:03:00.889 --> 00:03:01.973
Just a little touch.

00:03:01.973 --> 00:03:02.599
- All right, man.

00:03:03.266 --> 00:03:04.267
We gonna have a conversation or

00:03:04.267 --> 00:03:06.477
you just gonna play the whole time?

00:03:06.477 --> 00:03:09.689
- Oh, no, man. I'm witchu. Let's do it.

00:03:09.689 --> 00:03:10.190
- Seven in the side.
- Oh, you good.

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:11.065
Go ahead.

00:03:11.065 --> 00:03:11.941
- I can talk and play.

00:03:13.943 --> 00:03:14.527
Talk and play pool.

00:03:14.527 --> 00:03:15.528
I don't talk and play football.

00:03:16.154 --> 00:03:17.864
I ain't got time for that. I'll leave that for

00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:18.448
the divas.

00:03:18.531 --> 00:03:19.657
- I'm, I'mma go ahead and let you hit this

00:03:19.657 --> 00:03:20.783
five ball, but I'mma start this conversation.

00:03:25.288 --> 00:03:27.498
Before we start to talk to you about your career,

00:03:27.498 --> 00:03:29.334
uh, the sports world been struck

00:03:29.334 --> 00:03:30.335
with tragedy this week.

00:03:30.793 --> 00:03:31.002
- Yeah.

00:03:31.002 --> 00:03:32.462
- With the passing of Kobe Bryant, not only just

00:03:32.462 --> 00:03:34.130
in the sports world, but we've seen how

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:35.590
it's impacted everyone around the world.

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:36.049
- Yeah.

00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:37.884
- Wanted to get your thoughts on, on where

00:03:37.884 --> 00:03:39.427
you were on Sunday and how that has

00:03:39.427 --> 00:03:40.470
impacted you this week.

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:43.056
- First, I mean, prayers out to all that

00:03:43.056 --> 00:03:45.350
was, um, affected. All the families that

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:47.810
was affected and the Bryant family.

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:50.188
You know, um, it's tough, man.

00:03:50.188 --> 00:03:52.357
You know, I always say, none of us know

00:03:52.357 --> 00:03:53.316
the day or the hour,

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:56.653
um, when, when God would call us home. You

00:03:56.653 --> 00:03:59.948
know, this is, uh, this place we're in,

00:03:59.948 --> 00:04:00.907
man, it's rented.

00:04:00.907 --> 00:04:02.158
We rentin' this place.

00:04:02.158 --> 00:04:04.035
You know, this is not our true home,

00:04:04.035 --> 00:04:05.495
you know? And I, you know, when, when

00:04:05.495 --> 00:04:08.414
somebody passes, it's, it's very unfortunate,

00:04:08.414 --> 00:04:09.832
you know, especially when it's young and

00:04:09.832 --> 00:04:11.876
a mother and dad gotta bury

00:04:11.876 --> 00:04:15.588
their parents, or, you know, a daughter.

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:17.382
You know, and the husband, man, that,

00:04:17.382 --> 00:04:19.634
that's, that's, that's very hard to deal

00:04:19.634 --> 00:04:21.344
with, you know? But we're left here with

00:04:21.344 --> 00:04:24.013
the memories that we won't have, and that

00:04:24.013 --> 00:04:25.348
we, we hold on to it.

00:04:25.348 --> 00:04:26.933
And that's the, that's the hardest part,

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:27.642
you know?

00:04:27.642 --> 00:04:29.936
But all we, all we take from this place is

00:04:29.936 --> 00:04:30.478
memories.

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:30.853
- Yeah.

00:04:30.853 --> 00:04:31.604
- That's all you take.

00:04:31.604 --> 00:04:32.313
when you leave, man.

00:04:32.313 --> 00:04:35.316
You ain't takin' no money, ain't takin' no jewelry.

00:04:35.316 --> 00:04:36.859
All you takin' is the memories,

00:04:36.859 --> 00:04:37.443
you know?

00:04:37.443 --> 00:04:38.945
- Did you have any memories with Kobe?
- Or should I say you're leavin',

00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:39.570
you're leavin' 'em.

00:04:40.071 --> 00:04:42.073
I mean, I grew up watching Kobe.

00:04:42.073 --> 00:04:44.367
I'm the same age as Kobe, man. Forty-one,

00:04:44.367 --> 00:04:46.369
you know? And where I'm from, you don't

00:04:46.369 --> 00:04:48.371
make it past 14, you know?

00:04:48.371 --> 00:04:51.416
So I'm grateful to be where I'm at right

00:04:51.416 --> 00:04:52.166
now, man.

00:04:52.166 --> 00:04:55.169
But, um, cuz I grew up watchin' Kobe, man,

00:04:55.169 --> 00:04:57.588
since he'd been in the league, since he was

00:04:57.588 --> 00:04:58.589
in high school.

00:04:58.589 --> 00:04:59.048
You know?

00:04:59.048 --> 00:05:01.634
I mean, how do you not, you know, watch

00:05:01.634 --> 00:05:03.678
what he's done on and off the court?

00:05:03.678 --> 00:05:05.096
You know, I'm a big philanthropist.

00:05:05.138 --> 00:05:06.931
I love workin' with kids.

00:05:06.931 --> 00:05:09.350
And, and the things he was doin', man, how

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:12.270
he's transcended the sport, you know, as a

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:14.564
human being off the court, man, how you

00:05:14.564 --> 00:05:15.606
not watch that?

00:05:15.606 --> 00:05:17.650
You know, how you not emulate that?

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:19.986
You know, I always looked up to the greats.

00:05:19.986 --> 00:05:21.404
You know, Michael Jordan was one of them,

00:05:21.446 --> 00:05:22.905
was my favorite player.

00:05:22.905 --> 00:05:25.658
And, and knowing that Kobe was, Kobe was

00:05:25.658 --> 00:05:27.785
everything, just like Mike, you know?

00:05:27.785 --> 00:05:30.288
And, um, that, that's the feeling I thought.

00:05:30.288 --> 00:05:32.248
Like, man, that's like losing Michael Jordan.

00:05:32.248 --> 00:05:33.041
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:05:33.041 --> 00:05:34.334
That's losing the great players.

00:05:34.334 --> 00:05:35.585
Muhammad Ali,

00:05:35.585 --> 00:05:38.921
you know? So, very, very sad

00:05:38.921 --> 00:05:41.966
um, day for the world, you know? And it

00:05:41.966 --> 00:05:43.676
just so happened I woke up and read

00:05:43.676 --> 00:05:48.181
Proverbs 1—Proverbs 27:1, on that

00:05:48.181 --> 00:05:50.600
morning, you know? And I actually put it

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:52.977
in the, um, Instagram today, just Proverbs

00:05:52.977 --> 00:05:53.269
1.

00:05:53.269 --> 00:05:55.271
You read that, you know what I'm sayin'?

00:05:55.271 --> 00:05:57.982
Man, it just, it helps me, you know,

00:05:57.982 --> 00:06:00.485
because when, when you experience that,

00:06:00.485 --> 00:06:03.821
all of us bein' that it's, you know,

00:06:03.821 --> 00:06:04.697
Kobe.

00:06:05.656 --> 00:06:07.533
If you experienced that in the last year,

00:06:07.533 --> 00:06:08.701
two years, five years—

00:06:08.701 --> 00:06:09.952
we deal with it different—

00:06:09.952 --> 00:06:12.246
so it brings those feelings back.

00:06:12.246 --> 00:06:13.956
You know, I lost my brother when I was

00:06:13.956 --> 00:06:15.375
playin' in the league, man.

00:06:15.375 --> 00:06:17.710
And, um, I know how tough that is.

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:20.380
You know, I've been just small texting

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:22.507
with LeBron, just, you know, givin' him

00:06:22.507 --> 00:06:26.636
words and encouragement and, um, you know,

00:06:26.636 --> 00:06:28.888
you just never know what somebody's goin'

00:06:28.888 --> 00:06:29.347
through, man.

00:06:29.347 --> 00:06:30.973
So I just wanted to reach out and just

00:06:30.973 --> 00:06:33.101
say, you know, "I know it's hard, man, but

00:06:33.101 --> 00:06:33.851
stay strong."

00:06:33.851 --> 00:06:34.268
You know?

00:06:34.936 --> 00:06:36.729
- Everyone knew about the Mamba Mentality and

00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:38.189
the work ethic that he put in, not only on

00:06:38.189 --> 00:06:39.732
the court, but in everything that he

00:06:39.732 --> 00:06:40.775
applied himself to do.

00:06:41.692 --> 00:06:43.486
I think we also know you for your work

00:06:43.486 --> 00:06:45.696
ethic as well, and, and the,

00:06:45.696 --> 00:06:47.198
the effort and the dedication and the

00:06:47.198 --> 00:06:48.491
attention to detail that you put into

00:06:48.491 --> 00:06:50.618
everything that you, that you apply yourself to.

00:06:50.618 --> 00:06:51.160
- Yeah.

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:51.911
Where did that come from?

00:06:52.495 --> 00:06:54.580
- Man, that come from my parents.

00:06:54.705 --> 00:06:58.000
You know, um, I'm, I'm their seed, you

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:00.503
know? It's in them, you know? But

00:07:00.503 --> 00:07:02.046
I had a lotta mentors too, man.

00:07:02.046 --> 00:07:04.340
You gotta have a great circle of people

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:08.469
around you if you wanna be successful in

00:07:08.469 --> 00:07:11.514
life, I think. Um, if you wanna achieve

00:07:11.514 --> 00:07:12.932
great things, you gotta have the right

00:07:12.932 --> 00:07:14.308
people in your circle.

00:07:14.308 --> 00:07:15.726
It's who you surround yourself with.

00:07:15.726 --> 00:07:17.937
I tell this to kids all the time: "You show

00:07:17.979 --> 00:07:18.563
me your friends,

00:07:18.563 --> 00:07:19.730
I'll tell you who you are."
- True.

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:20.565
You know, me and

00:07:20.565 --> 00:07:22.942
Mr. George was talking about that last night.

00:07:22.942 --> 00:07:24.902
you know, man? And that's truly what it

00:07:24.902 --> 00:07:25.153
is.

00:07:25.153 --> 00:07:27.530
I've always surrounded myself around

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:29.574
God, God-fearing people,

00:07:29.574 --> 00:07:32.535
you know, and, and follow God in people.

00:07:32.535 --> 00:07:35.079
You know, that was, that was a big part of

00:07:35.079 --> 00:07:37.623
just livin', you know? My, my parents

00:07:37.623 --> 00:07:38.666
worked hard, man.

00:07:38.666 --> 00:07:42.295
My dad worked at Avondale Shipyard for 30 years.

00:07:42.295 --> 00:07:45.423
My mom worked in kitchens, you know, for

00:07:45.423 --> 00:07:46.591
20-plus years.

00:07:46.591 --> 00:07:48.926
And that's the reason why I cook, you know?

00:07:48.926 --> 00:07:51.387
So I mean, just the things I knew, they

00:07:51.387 --> 00:07:51.971
went through me.

00:07:51.971 --> 00:07:54.849
I feel like kids are supposed to take over

00:07:54.849 --> 00:07:56.893
where their parents left off.

00:07:56.893 --> 00:07:59.854
You know, parents build it to a certain point.

00:07:59.854 --> 00:08:02.648
Kids supposed to be workin' out, training,

00:08:02.648 --> 00:08:04.692
playing sports, goin' to school, gettin'

00:08:04.692 --> 00:08:06.694
good grades, and when they get old enough,

00:08:06.694 --> 00:08:09.238
you're supposed to take where your parents

00:08:09.238 --> 00:08:10.948
left off and build up on that.

00:08:10.948 --> 00:08:13.826
Not go your ass back home and puttin' the

00:08:13.826 --> 00:08:15.369
milk, puttin' your name on the milk and

00:08:15.369 --> 00:08:16.162
stuff like that.

00:08:16.204 --> 00:08:17.830
You know, that's not what it's about.

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:20.082
You know, I really believe your parents,

00:08:20.082 --> 00:08:21.876
and I always say this to my kid and say it to

00:08:21.876 --> 00:08:23.294
a bunch of kids, your parents are raisin'

00:08:23.294 --> 00:08:25.171
you to leave the house, not stay.

00:08:25.171 --> 00:08:27.298
- They don't want you back in there.
- True story.

00:08:28.174 --> 00:08:29.759
- Lemme see if I got any shots right here real quick.

00:08:29.759 --> 00:08:31.135
- Yeah, you make shots, man.

00:08:31.135 --> 00:08:32.428
I'm a defensive guy.

00:08:32.428 --> 00:08:34.138
I'm a defensive guy, man.
- You said I was right?

00:08:34.138 --> 00:08:36.140
- There's a lotta blue in between here.

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:36.933
- Oh, you think I'm scared?

00:08:36.933 --> 00:08:37.558
- Yeah.

00:08:37.558 --> 00:08:39.018
Lemme get this cigar goin'.

00:08:39.018 --> 00:08:40.019
- Go ahead, get that goin' while I'm gettin' this shot.

00:08:40.019 --> 00:08:42.688
- Shouts out to the fans that's over here.

00:08:42.688 --> 00:08:43.689
- They got a couple folks out here, man.

00:08:43.689 --> 00:08:45.775
I didn't know y'all was gonna have a live audience.

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:49.070
- We gotta do it right for you, man.

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:50.696
- I feel like I'm shootin' one of the "Martin"

00:08:50.696 --> 00:08:51.531
shows or something.

00:08:52.949 --> 00:08:54.408
- Who, uh, who taught you the game of football?

00:08:54.408 --> 00:08:54.867
Where'd you learn?

00:08:55.493 --> 00:08:57.495
- Man, when I was born, man. I mean, I

00:08:57.495 --> 00:08:59.747
always say I was born to play football.

00:09:00.081 --> 00:09:02.166
You know, it was just in me. You know, my

00:09:02.166 --> 00:09:04.460
dad was a super athlete, played everything.

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:06.462
I played every sport: baseball,

00:09:06.462 --> 00:09:09.340
basketball, ran track, soccer, you know.

00:09:09.340 --> 00:09:11.259
So I always said I was born to play

00:09:11.259 --> 00:09:12.552
football, you know?

00:09:12.552 --> 00:09:14.637
And truly it was one of the only ways out

00:09:14.637 --> 00:09:17.640
of my neighborhood in Metarie, you know what I'm sayin'? So,

00:09:18.432 --> 00:09:20.935
when I was five years old, my brother

00:09:20.935 --> 00:09:22.728
tossed me the ball and was like, "Get past me."

00:09:22.728 --> 00:09:25.648
My older brother, you know? I played a lotta

00:09:25.648 --> 00:09:27.149
street football,

00:09:27.149 --> 00:09:27.775
you know what I'm sayin'?

00:09:27.775 --> 00:09:29.902
Pole to pole, lamppost to lamppost,

00:09:29.902 --> 00:09:30.945
mailbox to mailbox.

00:09:30.945 --> 00:09:33.030
Cuz I got a scar on my head from runnin' into

00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:35.366
a mailbox when I was, like, 12 years old.

00:09:35.366 --> 00:09:36.617
I caught the ball too.

00:09:36.617 --> 00:09:37.326
- How did that happen?

00:09:37.326 --> 00:09:38.619
I gotta, I gotta hear this story. Because I

00:09:38.619 --> 00:09:40.580
know that your Hall of Fame bust, everyone

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:42.331
always talks about how beautiful that

00:09:42.331 --> 00:09:44.584
bust is, the attention to detail to that bust.

00:09:44.584 --> 00:09:46.836
- Man, I gotta picture when I was, like,

00:09:48.004 --> 00:09:49.088
seven years old.

00:09:49.088 --> 00:09:51.549
I always had hair on my head, but, um,

00:09:51.549 --> 00:09:53.509
bein' that football is such a physical

00:09:53.509 --> 00:09:56.596
sport, you know, I, um, I felt like havin'

00:09:56.596 --> 00:09:58.723
a lil' cushion, you know what I'm sayin',

00:09:58.723 --> 00:09:59.890
would help me out.

00:09:59.890 --> 00:10:02.852
You know, you get—football is a violent sport.

00:10:02.852 --> 00:10:05.313
A lotta concussions, stingers and such

00:10:05.313 --> 00:10:06.063
like that, man.

00:10:06.063 --> 00:10:08.774
So, I know it's just—it was in my nature.

00:10:08.774 --> 00:10:10.568
It's part of my culture, you know?

00:10:10.568 --> 00:10:12.820
Um, you know, I see like, like we're

00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:14.071
dealin' with a lotta

00:10:14.947 --> 00:10:16.699
ignorant stuff these days, man.

00:10:16.699 --> 00:10:18.242
Especially when it comes to kids and

00:10:18.242 --> 00:10:20.244
school and their hair, you know? Like this

00:10:20.244 --> 00:10:22.663
one kid in Texas, they askin' the kid to

00:10:22.663 --> 00:10:23.372
cut his hair.
- Dreadlocks.

00:10:23.372 --> 00:10:23.998
- Yeah, man.
- It's crazy.

00:10:23.998 --> 00:10:25.750
- The kid been wearin' 'em since—for a long time.

00:10:25.750 --> 00:10:27.877
Like, that has nothin' to do with school.

00:10:28.252 --> 00:10:29.754
But that's the ignorance that we're

00:10:29.754 --> 00:10:30.713
dealing with, you know?

00:10:30.713 --> 00:10:33.924
So for me to wear like this, you know, I

00:10:33.924 --> 00:10:36.010
know I need to cut it at times, but

00:10:36.010 --> 00:10:37.428
it's just natural to me, man.

00:10:37.428 --> 00:10:39.221
I'm comfortable in my own skin, and

00:10:39.221 --> 00:10:39.972
that's what it's about.

00:10:39.972 --> 00:10:42.016
You gotta be comfortable with who you are.

00:10:42.725 --> 00:10:43.809
- So I gotta hear the mailbox story.

00:10:43.809 --> 00:10:45.394
OK, so you caught the pass, but what happened?

00:10:45.394 --> 00:10:46.729
How—did you see the mailbox?

00:10:46.729 --> 00:10:47.855
Was it yo—oh, you gettin' fancy.

00:10:47.855 --> 00:10:49.565
- No, I ain't see the mailbox.
- You doin' the Martin Luther King

00:10:49.565 --> 00:10:51.275
shot right here on me right now, man.

00:10:51.275 --> 00:10:52.735
- I am Martin Luther King.

00:10:53.944 --> 00:10:57.365
Part of. Um, naw, man, we was playin' on this

00:10:57.365 --> 00:11:00.284
spot called the playground, and the guy

00:11:00.284 --> 00:11:02.370
threw the ball, and I'm runnin' like this.

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:04.664
I'm runnin', lookin' for the ball, but

00:11:04.664 --> 00:11:06.582
it's, you know, it's—I'm outrunnin' the

00:11:06.582 --> 00:11:07.375
ball basically.

00:11:07.375 --> 00:11:09.335
So I'm lookin' at it like this right here.

00:11:09.335 --> 00:11:10.628
And as I catch the ball,

00:11:11.337 --> 00:11:14.465
I hit the mailbox. And you know how kids

00:11:14.465 --> 00:11:16.258
are. I got the ball in my hand. They like,

00:11:16.258 --> 00:11:17.760
"Let us see! Let us see!"

00:11:18.386 --> 00:11:20.137
And I moved my hand, and nothin' but blood.

00:11:20.137 --> 00:11:22.973
I passed out after that. My dad did all—

00:11:22.973 --> 00:11:24.475
next thing I heard was like, "Your dad's

00:11:24.475 --> 00:11:26.602
goin' your way, on his way to come get you."

00:11:26.602 --> 00:11:26.936
You know?

00:11:26.936 --> 00:11:28.938
So that probably was my first concussion, man.

00:11:30.398 --> 00:11:32.274
- You get stitches or surgery? What—

00:11:32.274 --> 00:11:33.442
- I had stitches.

00:11:33.442 --> 00:11:35.736
I had stiitches. I had, like, 16 stitches, man.

00:11:35.736 --> 00:11:36.987
- And how long did it take for it to heal up?

00:11:39.365 --> 00:11:40.533
- For life.

00:11:40.533 --> 00:11:41.784
I'm still bruised.

00:11:43.244 --> 00:11:43.828
- I gotta ask you:

00:11:43.828 --> 00:11:45.371
So you, you're a guy who grows up in the

00:11:45.371 --> 00:11:45.955
New Orleans area.

00:11:45.955 --> 00:11:47.498
It feels like you were born to play football.

00:11:48.332 --> 00:11:49.333
Why did you choose Miami?

00:11:53.129 --> 00:11:54.964
- Y'all might wanna stand up and look and

00:11:54.964 --> 00:11:56.298
say, "Why not Miami, right?"

00:11:57.633 --> 00:11:59.385
Go stand by them stairs, man.

00:12:00.803 --> 00:12:02.513
I mean, I wanted to get away from home.

00:12:02.930 --> 00:12:03.723
You know what I'm saying?

00:12:03.723 --> 00:12:06.892
I felt like my, my environment was not,

00:12:06.892 --> 00:12:09.812
um, not healthy enough for me to do the

00:12:09.812 --> 00:12:11.188
things that I wanted to do.

00:12:11.188 --> 00:12:15.151
Um, I grew up around—on my way to school, man,

00:12:15.151 --> 00:12:18.529
I'd pass up three plantations on my way to

00:12:18.529 --> 00:12:19.113
high school—
- Wow.

00:12:19.113 --> 00:12:21.198
- I pass up three plantations.

00:12:21.198 --> 00:12:24.910
You know, I do—I did a golf tournament

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:26.537
five minutes from my high school,

00:12:26.537 --> 00:12:28.372
not even. You could walk to this golf

00:12:28.372 --> 00:12:30.624
course. In 1990,

00:12:30.624 --> 00:12:31.917
my mentor,

00:12:32.793 --> 00:12:34.795
they were still not able to go to this

00:12:34.795 --> 00:12:36.505
golf course as black people.

00:12:36.505 --> 00:12:38.924
You talkin' in the '90s, you know what

00:12:38.924 --> 00:12:39.258
I'm sayin'?

00:12:39.258 --> 00:12:42.887
So I just didn't wanna be around people

00:12:42.887 --> 00:12:46.891
who just had that old-school mentality,

00:12:46.891 --> 00:12:47.767
you know what I'm sayin'?

00:12:47.767 --> 00:12:50.227
Like, that's still in the South, you know?

00:12:50.227 --> 00:12:51.604
You still see it in Mississippi.

00:12:51.604 --> 00:12:53.105
My people were from Mississippi.

00:12:53.105 --> 00:12:54.982
My family's from Mississippi.
- With the

00:12:54.982 --> 00:12:56.025
prison reform situation and all that.

00:12:56.025 --> 00:12:57.067
- Yeah.
- Absolutely.

00:12:57.067 --> 00:12:57.777
- You know what I'm sayin'?

00:12:57.777 --> 00:12:58.736
So,

00:12:58.736 --> 00:12:59.487
that stuff,

00:12:59.487 --> 00:13:00.780
that stuff means somethin' to me, man.

00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:01.781
I'm a people's person.

00:13:01.781 --> 00:13:05.367
I used to live with my, my, um, secretary

00:13:05.367 --> 00:13:06.410
of my high school.

00:13:06.410 --> 00:13:10.039
She's a white lady, you know? Her family's

00:13:10.039 --> 00:13:13.167
Caucasian, white, you know? So I lived with them.

00:13:13.167 --> 00:13:14.460
That's my family.

00:13:14.752 --> 00:13:15.377
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:13:15.377 --> 00:13:16.378
So I saw things—

00:13:16.378 --> 00:13:17.630
I see things different, man.

00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:19.799
I'm not—I don't, I don't—I'm not out

00:13:19.799 --> 00:13:22.134
here judgin' people and "You're racist."

00:13:22.134 --> 00:13:23.219
I don't do that,

00:13:23.219 --> 00:13:23.928
you know?

00:13:25.137 --> 00:13:26.347
It is what it is.

00:13:26.347 --> 00:13:28.516
But I wanted to get away from that, man,

00:13:28.516 --> 00:13:31.018
and I felt like—I felt like Miami was

00:13:31.018 --> 00:13:33.270
gonna be different. And come to find out

00:13:33.270 --> 00:13:36.482
it wasn't too much of a difference, you know?

00:13:36.482 --> 00:13:38.984
- Ain't that the truth?
- But, but I really wanted—I mean, I wanted to

00:13:38.984 --> 00:13:41.320
win a national championship, you know?

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:42.947
I wanted, I wanted to win a national

00:13:42.947 --> 00:13:44.907
championship, and thank God we did.

00:13:44.907 --> 00:13:46.575
You know, I actually got my ring on right

00:13:46.575 --> 00:13:49.662
here. You know, so that, that's what it was about

00:13:49.662 --> 00:13:52.081
to me, man. It was about playin', playin'

00:13:52.081 --> 00:13:53.999
at the highest level, but puttin' myself in

00:13:53.999 --> 00:13:56.961
position to also make it to the NFL.

00:13:57.044 --> 00:13:58.838
You know, I was a businessman at a young

00:13:58.838 --> 00:13:59.880
age, man.

00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:02.424
- That 2001 team, considered one of the

00:14:02.424 --> 00:14:04.969
greatest, if not the greatest. You think

00:14:04.969 --> 00:14:05.553
it's the greatest?

00:14:06.178 --> 00:14:06.679
- Yeah.

00:14:07.179 --> 00:14:08.138
Yeah. In college football?

00:14:08.138 --> 00:14:09.306
- We got some Canes in the house over here.

00:14:09.306 --> 00:14:10.474
In college football?

00:14:12.184 --> 00:14:14.228
I mean, I don't have to, I don't have to

00:14:14.228 --> 00:14:15.020
shout out the records.

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:16.188
I don't have to shout out the

00:14:16.188 --> 00:14:17.565
first-rounders, but I'mma just talk about

00:14:17.565 --> 00:14:19.441
two people: myself and Sean Taylor.

00:14:19.441 --> 00:14:21.443
Sean Taylor was my backup.

00:14:21.443 --> 00:14:23.487
He wasn't even starting then, and he's a

00:14:23.487 --> 00:14:26.156
beast. Hall of Famer in my eyes, you know

00:14:26.156 --> 00:14:26.991
what I'm sayin'?

00:14:26.991 --> 00:14:28.200
Antrel Rolle, those guys like that.

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:30.828
Jonathan Vilma, all first-rounders, you

00:14:30.828 --> 00:14:31.370
know what I'm sayin'?

00:14:31.370 --> 00:14:36.834
So we, we—we was on another level in

00:14:36.834 --> 00:14:41.422
college, but it was about our coaching staff.

00:14:41.422 --> 00:14:43.465
That's what people don't talk about.

00:14:43.465 --> 00:14:45.384
It's the people who have guided you, man.

00:14:45.384 --> 00:14:47.428
Everybody should have a mentor,

00:14:47.428 --> 00:14:48.053
you know what I'm sayin'?

00:14:48.053 --> 00:14:51.473
But we had men raising young men, you know

00:14:51.473 --> 00:14:51.891
what I'm sayin'?

00:14:51.891 --> 00:14:53.434
And that's what it was about.

00:14:53.434 --> 00:14:55.394
You know, you gotta—you need guidance as

00:14:55.394 --> 00:14:57.521
a, as a young man, as a kid. When you get

00:14:57.521 --> 00:15:00.357
to college, women—young, young ladies

00:15:00.357 --> 00:15:02.818
and young men are becoming men now when

00:15:02.818 --> 00:15:04.695
they get to college, you know? It's the

00:15:04.695 --> 00:15:05.696
real world.

00:15:05.696 --> 00:15:06.780
- People growin' up so much faster now.

00:15:06.780 --> 00:15:07.281
- Exactly.

00:15:07.281 --> 00:15:08.157
Exactly.

00:15:08.157 --> 00:15:09.909
They have access to everything, right,

00:15:09.909 --> 00:15:10.910
at the touch of their hand.

00:15:10.910 --> 00:15:11.702
You know, so,

00:15:12.244 --> 00:15:12.786
I don't know, man.

00:15:12.786 --> 00:15:14.413
We just had a great coaching staff.

00:15:14.413 --> 00:15:16.332
We had Super Bowl-winning coaches: Coach

00:15:16.332 --> 00:15:20.085
Davis, Curtis Johnson, Chuck Pagano, Art Kehoe.

00:15:20.085 --> 00:15:21.253
I can name so many guys.

00:15:21.253 --> 00:15:23.797
Mario Cristobal, who's head coach at

00:15:23.797 --> 00:15:24.340
Oregon now.

00:15:24.340 --> 00:15:27.551
We had a lotta great men leading young

00:15:27.551 --> 00:15:29.136
men, and that's what we need now.

00:15:29.136 --> 00:15:32.139
We need us men to lead our men,

00:15:32.139 --> 00:15:32.765
you know what I'm sayin'?

00:15:32.765 --> 00:15:34.934
Lead these young men, these young kids.

00:15:34.934 --> 00:15:36.185
We need that more.

00:15:36.226 --> 00:15:38.020
You see the women doin' it.

00:15:38.020 --> 00:15:39.229
Women gonna be overtakin' over

00:15:39.229 --> 00:15:41.106
this world sooner than later.

00:15:41.106 --> 00:15:42.191
I mean, I'm with that though.

00:15:42.191 --> 00:15:43.776
I mean, we gonna have us a woman

00:15:43.776 --> 00:15:45.736
president, you know, runnin' this thing.

00:15:45.736 --> 00:15:48.405
Because, I mean, they, they stand together,

00:15:48.405 --> 00:15:49.990
you know what I mean? They stick together.

00:15:49.990 --> 00:15:52.409
You see all kinds of women groups doin'

00:15:52.409 --> 00:15:53.702
all kinds of things, man.

00:15:54.119 --> 00:15:55.788
You know, and we need to, we need to stand

00:15:55.788 --> 00:15:58.958
up as men and, and take hold of

00:15:58.958 --> 00:16:01.418
what we supposed to do too.
- Is it your shot?

00:16:01.919 --> 00:16:02.711
- Yeah, it's still my shot.

00:16:03.003 --> 00:16:03.295
- Go ahead.

00:16:03.963 --> 00:16:06.048
- I don't have nothin'.
- I don't think you gonna make anything, yeah.

00:16:06.048 --> 00:16:07.466
Cuz I ain't made nothin' yet.

00:16:07.466 --> 00:16:09.051
- I'll give you a shot now.

00:16:09.051 --> 00:16:11.178
- Go ahead.
- Somewhat.

00:16:13.013 --> 00:16:13.514
- Thank you.

00:16:13.514 --> 00:16:15.474
- I'll help you out.
- This is a charity donation.

00:16:15.474 --> 00:16:16.100
- I'll help you out.

00:16:16.642 --> 00:16:18.018
- Now, look, I went to Arizona State.

00:16:18.018 --> 00:16:19.019
I also grew up in Texas.

00:16:19.019 --> 00:16:21.355
Wanted to escape my situation as well, and

00:16:21.355 --> 00:16:23.273
I went—had the same experience you did in Miami.

00:16:23.273 --> 00:16:24.108
I was like, "Why the hell would I wanna

00:16:24.108 --> 00:16:24.775
go anywhere else?"

00:16:25.401 --> 00:16:27.152
Arizona State is a party school.

00:16:27.152 --> 00:16:29.822
I had a good time in my four years, but I wasn't

00:16:30.614 --> 00:16:32.574
a prominent football player at the

00:16:32.574 --> 00:16:34.076
University of Miami.
- Right.

00:16:34.076 --> 00:16:35.995
- What was the party scene like?

00:16:35.995 --> 00:16:37.788
What was it like bein' the man on campus

00:16:37.788 --> 00:16:38.872
and the man in Miami?

00:16:39.415 --> 00:16:41.208
- The party scene could wait.

00:16:41.208 --> 00:16:43.127
I never came to the beach when I was in college.

00:16:43.752 --> 00:16:46.171
I never crossed that bridge when I was in college.

00:16:46.171 --> 00:16:50.509
Um, you know, I, I understood to do the

00:16:50.509 --> 00:16:51.719
things that I wanted to do, man,

00:16:51.719 --> 00:16:53.554
I needed to be around my teammates and

00:16:53.554 --> 00:16:55.055
stay close to that school.

00:16:55.055 --> 00:16:55.889
Don't get me wrong.

00:16:55.889 --> 00:16:57.891
We had some times when we,

00:16:57.891 --> 00:16:59.518
you know, when I got—I was older, man. I

00:16:59.518 --> 00:17:02.646
was 21, 22 when I graduated, you know? I

00:17:02.646 --> 00:17:03.689
stayed five years.

00:17:03.689 --> 00:17:07.776
So it's how you handle things, man, at the

00:17:07.776 --> 00:17:08.694
end of the day.

00:17:08.694 --> 00:17:09.528
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:17:09.528 --> 00:17:12.406
I had a lotta fun, but when I got to my

00:17:12.406 --> 00:17:13.532
senior year—we'll just talk about my

00:17:13.532 --> 00:17:19.413
senior year. We didn't party during the week.

00:17:19.413 --> 00:17:20.789
We partied after the game.

00:17:20.789 --> 00:17:22.583
We can get—you can do whatever you want after

00:17:22.583 --> 00:17:24.793
the game, but that week, man, just chill out.

00:17:24.793 --> 00:17:25.544
- Why?

00:17:25.544 --> 00:17:27.337
- Because there's a focus time, man. It's

00:17:27.337 --> 00:17:28.338
work. You know,

00:17:28.338 --> 00:17:29.298
you got—you,

00:17:30.049 --> 00:17:31.675
you're playin' a physical sport.

00:17:31.675 --> 00:17:35.220
We put a lot into, as I say, I put my

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:39.099
heart in this shit. You know, in the

00:17:39.099 --> 00:17:40.267
offseason, as we did,

00:17:40.267 --> 00:17:42.394
let's not waste this opportunity.

00:17:43.103 --> 00:17:45.397
High school, college goes by like that.

00:17:45.397 --> 00:17:47.900
Even the career in the NFL, it goes by

00:17:47.900 --> 00:17:49.068
like that.

00:17:49.068 --> 00:17:51.403
I'm 41 years old, man,

00:17:51.403 --> 00:17:53.947
and retired from, from football.

00:17:53.947 --> 00:17:56.533
Like, I'm a retired—when people ask me what

00:17:56.533 --> 00:17:57.785
I do and go to the bank and I'll be like,

00:17:57.785 --> 00:17:58.243
"I'm retired."

00:17:58.243 --> 00:17:58.827
They'll be like, "What?

00:17:58.827 --> 00:17:59.661
Retired?"

00:18:01.121 --> 00:18:04.416
But now I say "Semiretired." Semiretired.

00:18:04.416 --> 00:18:07.086
Retired from football, but I still do

00:18:07.086 --> 00:18:07.753
other things.

00:18:07.753 --> 00:18:08.378
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:18:08.378 --> 00:18:10.714
So, you gotta have a plan, man.

00:18:10.714 --> 00:18:11.298
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:18:11.298 --> 00:18:15.010
So I knew that it was a short-lived

00:18:15.010 --> 00:18:17.096
career in, uh, in college.

00:18:17.096 --> 00:18:18.555
I knew it was gonna go by like that,

00:18:18.555 --> 00:18:19.389
and I wanted to enjoy it.

00:18:19.389 --> 00:18:21.016
I enjoyed every moment, man.

00:18:21.016 --> 00:18:22.684
- I gotta ask you about one specific moment while

00:18:22.684 --> 00:18:23.644
I take this shot real quick.

00:18:24.228 --> 00:18:26.563
I heard, Ed, you've got a story about

00:18:26.563 --> 00:18:28.482
Shannon Briggs. Who is Shannon Briggs?

00:18:28.857 --> 00:18:30.275
- Shannon Briggs.

00:18:31.193 --> 00:18:32.319
That's pretty funny right there, man.

00:18:33.153 --> 00:18:34.738
Shannon Briggs was a boxer.

00:18:34.947 --> 00:18:35.239
- Yeah?

00:18:35.405 --> 00:18:35.823
- Yeah.

00:18:35.823 --> 00:18:36.865
A champion boxer.

00:18:37.199 --> 00:18:37.533
- Word?

00:18:37.533 --> 00:18:38.784
- Very elite boxer.

00:18:38.784 --> 00:18:40.452
- How big we talkin' here?

00:18:40.452 --> 00:18:42.162
- Man, Shannon, Shannon Briggs, I mean, when

00:18:42.162 --> 00:18:44.832
he stood up, he looked like King Kong, but—

00:18:46.083 --> 00:18:47.334
About 6'5".
- That's a big-ass dude.

00:18:47.334 --> 00:18:48.627
- But he's huge, man.

00:18:48.627 --> 00:18:49.503
Solid, too.

00:18:49.503 --> 00:18:53.715
- When I say you solid, man—
- What, what happened?

00:18:53.715 --> 00:18:56.426
Word on the street is you and Shannon Briggs—

00:18:56.426 --> 00:18:57.094
- Whoa, whoa, whoa.

00:18:57.094 --> 00:18:58.846
Had a little somethin' somethin' that happened.

00:18:59.972 --> 00:19:02.099
What, what happened that night? And did you know

00:19:02.099 --> 00:19:03.183
he was even Shannon Briggs?

00:19:03.183 --> 00:19:03.934
- I didn't know.

00:19:03.934 --> 00:19:05.769
We didn't know he was Shannon Briggs. So—

00:19:05.769 --> 00:19:06.812
- What happened?

00:19:06.812 --> 00:19:09.148
- Long story short, we got this clique—

00:19:09.148 --> 00:19:09.982
- Nah, I need the long story.

00:19:09.982 --> 00:19:10.816
I need the long story.

00:19:10.816 --> 00:19:12.109
- All right, yeah, I'll give you the whole story.

00:19:12.442 --> 00:19:15.529
We got a group, the, the class of '95.

00:19:15.529 --> 00:19:18.574
I'm class of '97. Class of '95 was known as Two Gs cuz

00:19:18.574 --> 00:19:20.951
they graduate in 2000.
- Two Gs?

00:19:20.951 --> 00:19:21.743
- Two Gs.

00:19:21.743 --> 00:19:25.164
- OK.
- So, it's me and Bryant McKinnie.

00:19:25.164 --> 00:19:27.416
Bryant McKinnie and I are the youngest ones with

00:19:27.416 --> 00:19:29.418
them hangin' out, and we're on the beach.

00:19:29.418 --> 00:19:31.420
Now, we done cross the bridge, right?

00:19:31.879 --> 00:19:33.672
And that's why we never crossed the

00:19:33.672 --> 00:19:35.257
bridge. There's nothin' but trouble.

00:19:35.257 --> 00:19:38.177
So we come to the beach with the older guys.

00:19:38.177 --> 00:19:39.928
Me and Bryant McKinnie are the young guys. We're in the

00:19:39.928 --> 00:19:43.849
club, and one of the older guys come back

00:19:43.849 --> 00:19:46.018
in like, "Y'all gotta come on! Let's go!

00:19:46.018 --> 00:19:46.643
Let's go!

00:19:47.519 --> 00:19:49.438
Um, "One of our teammates just got knocked

00:19:49.438 --> 00:19:50.689
out!" Like, "Knocked out?!"

00:19:50.689 --> 00:19:51.773
"Yeah, he got in a fight.

00:19:51.773 --> 00:19:52.357
Let's go!"

00:19:52.357 --> 00:19:54.318
So we like, "All right, cool."

00:19:54.318 --> 00:19:55.527
We walkin' through the, um,

00:19:55.527 --> 00:19:58.030
we walked through the back of the club in

00:19:58.030 --> 00:19:59.364
the alley.

00:19:59.364 --> 00:20:01.283
Then we walked through the alley to Collins.

00:20:01.283 --> 00:20:03.285
As we comin' through, I'm actually in the

00:20:03.285 --> 00:20:07.247
front, and the, and the guy, you know, my man was

00:20:07.247 --> 00:20:08.415
like, "They right there."

00:20:08.415 --> 00:20:10.626
So I'm lookin'. I'm like, "So, what's up, man?"

00:20:10.626 --> 00:20:12.836
Like, "Why y'all jumped on our boy?"

00:20:12.836 --> 00:20:15.255
"Why, you know, why you knocked him out?"

00:20:15.255 --> 00:20:18.717
And lo and behold, he jumps up.

00:20:18.717 --> 00:20:20.427
"You want some?" Like Deebo.

00:20:21.595 --> 00:20:22.679
You want some too?"

00:20:22.679 --> 00:20:27.226
I'm like, "No, I don't! But I got a bunch of cash

00:20:27.226 --> 00:20:29.436
with me, so—"
- Who was with you?

00:20:29.436 --> 00:20:31.104
- Me, Bryant McKinnie, um...

00:20:31.104 --> 00:20:33.357
I'll just leave it at that—
- OK.

00:20:33.357 --> 00:20:36.401
- And, like, five other Two G guys.
- Once you said McKinnie, I knew—

00:20:36.401 --> 00:20:38.445
- Bryant McKinnie.
- You in good hands.

00:20:38.445 --> 00:20:42.616
- Because when he said that, and I'm like, "Nah,
man, we just tryna

00:20:42.616 --> 00:20:44.743
figure out." Bryant McKinnie come through.

00:20:44.743 --> 00:20:47.371
Bryant McKinnie is, if you had never saw Bryant

00:20:47.371 --> 00:20:48.538
McKinnie—
- How big is he?

00:20:48.538 --> 00:20:53.710
- Bryant McKinnie is, like, 6'9", 300-something at

00:20:53.710 --> 00:20:56.338
this time. Needless to say, Bryant McKinnie

00:20:56.338 --> 00:20:58.507
didn't give up a sack in Miami.

00:20:59.299 --> 00:21:03.053
So Bryant McKinnie comes through. "Wait, wait, wait!

00:21:03.053 --> 00:21:04.096
Get the—"

00:21:04.096 --> 00:21:05.597
He was like, "Get the little guy."

00:21:06.431 --> 00:21:08.600
So there's two of them, Shannon Briggs and

00:21:08.600 --> 00:21:09.393
the friend or whatever.

00:21:09.393 --> 00:21:13.522
So, I come around, whatever, hit the lil' guy.

00:21:13.522 --> 00:21:14.898
He's pretty much outta there.

00:21:16.483 --> 00:21:19.403
Bryant, Bryant grabbed Shannonn Briggs by his

00:21:19.403 --> 00:21:23.490
dreads. Yanks him down like he's Dwight Freeney.

00:21:23.490 --> 00:21:24.866
He's on the ground, man.

00:21:24.866 --> 00:21:25.826
And they just,

00:21:25.826 --> 00:21:27.452
they whalin' on him now.

00:21:27.452 --> 00:21:29.871
I mean, they are goin' to town on him,

00:21:29.871 --> 00:21:30.789
right?

00:21:30.789 --> 00:21:33.041
And he's just on the ground on his all

00:21:33.041 --> 00:21:36.003
fours just takin' it. "Ooh, ooh."
Right?

00:21:37.504 --> 00:21:38.380
So I go over there.

00:21:38.380 --> 00:21:39.965
By the time I get over there, man, this

00:21:39.965 --> 00:21:42.634
guy's just took off runnin' outta the group.

00:21:42.634 --> 00:21:43.593
He's gettin' kicked,

00:21:43.593 --> 00:21:45.887
all kinds of stuff, right?

00:21:45.887 --> 00:21:48.348
So we go back in the club. I'm like, "Man, I'm done.

00:21:48.348 --> 00:21:49.766
I'm ready to go home now, man."

00:21:49.766 --> 00:21:51.727
We gotta find our boy though.

00:21:51.727 --> 00:21:53.228
So we finally get him.

00:21:53.228 --> 00:21:54.730
We're on—

00:21:54.730 --> 00:22:00.027
we're, like, Collins and 17th.

00:22:00.027 --> 00:22:00.944
- So how far is that from here,

00:22:00.944 --> 00:22:01.737
right in this location

00:22:01.737 --> 00:22:03.238
we're in in South Beach right now?

00:22:03.238 --> 00:22:06.074
It's not even two streets over. Like, we're on 17th right now.

00:22:06.074 --> 00:22:09.369
Like, this is 17th. We're, like, right here, and he's at

00:22:09.369 --> 00:22:10.829
Nikki Beach.

00:22:10.829 --> 00:22:17.294
Nikki Beach is, is um, Ocean and 1. You

00:22:17.294 --> 00:22:18.337
know, Ocean and 1st.

00:22:18.337 --> 00:22:20.088
And you like, "What—how do you get down

00:22:20.088 --> 00:22:21.131
there that quick?"

00:22:21.131 --> 00:22:22.549
He ran down there, dude.

00:22:24.593 --> 00:22:26.595
Like, Shannon Briggs kicked your ass.

00:22:27.637 --> 00:22:29.598
You know, so that, I mean, that's kinda

00:22:29.598 --> 00:22:30.265
how it happened, man.

00:22:30.265 --> 00:22:33.185
That next day, dude, we had practice.

00:22:33.185 --> 00:22:35.812
We walkin' out there sore. Like, we got in a

00:22:35.812 --> 00:22:36.646
boxing fight.

00:22:36.646 --> 00:22:38.607
We didn't know that it was Shannon Briggs

00:22:38.607 --> 00:22:39.649
at the time.

00:22:39.649 --> 00:22:40.275
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:22:40.275 --> 00:22:43.153
Until he actually got sued by my boy

00:22:43.153 --> 00:22:44.029
cuz he broke his jaw.

00:22:44.029 --> 00:22:46.615
You know, you can't—boxers can't fight on the street.

00:22:46.615 --> 00:22:48.533
Those are weapons, you know, so he wound

00:22:48.533 --> 00:22:49.785
up gettin' sued

00:22:49.785 --> 00:22:50.452
and everything.

00:22:50.452 --> 00:22:51.119
You know what I'm sayin', so.

00:22:51.119 --> 00:22:53.330
- So are you cool with Shannon Briggs now?

00:22:53.330 --> 00:22:55.540
What's the relationship—
- I never even met him man!

00:22:55.540 --> 00:22:56.917
Never even saw him after that.

00:22:57.918 --> 00:22:59.961
And I hope you not watchin' this right now.

00:23:03.173 --> 00:23:05.008
- We'll take that from, from there and go

00:23:05.008 --> 00:23:06.051
straight to the NFL,

00:23:06.051 --> 00:23:06.385
OK?

00:23:07.344 --> 00:23:09.554
You're finishing up your rookie year in

00:23:09.554 --> 00:23:10.722
the NFL with the Ravens.

00:23:11.681 --> 00:23:13.558
You say you flipped a switch after your

00:23:13.558 --> 00:23:14.017
rookie year.

00:23:14.017 --> 00:23:15.435
Why?
- Yeah.

00:23:15.435 --> 00:23:18.480
Cuz I, I understood to have—

00:23:19.564 --> 00:23:21.733
when you get to the NFL, you hear, "Not for

00:23:21.733 --> 00:23:24.361
long," and it's true, man.

00:23:24.361 --> 00:23:25.904
- Average NFL career's, what, three seasons?

00:23:25.904 --> 00:23:26.113
- Yeah,

00:23:26.113 --> 00:23:27.531
that's because the numbers are skewed

00:23:27.531 --> 00:23:29.699
because of free agents comin' in and out

00:23:29.699 --> 00:23:30.367
all the time.

00:23:30.367 --> 00:23:32.619
It's a cutthroat business, man. I always said

00:23:32.619 --> 00:23:33.787
it was like the drug game.

00:23:33.787 --> 00:23:35.288
They don't care.

00:23:35.288 --> 00:23:36.706
They get rid of you in a minute.

00:23:36.706 --> 00:23:40.710
So, when I got there, you hear "Not for long."

00:23:41.503 --> 00:23:43.338
I was like, "No, I need—a need for

00:23:43.338 --> 00:23:44.381
longevity."

00:23:44.965 --> 00:23:45.757
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:23:45.757 --> 00:23:47.759
Like, "I wanna get longevity out of this.

00:23:47.759 --> 00:23:48.635
I wanna have a career.

00:23:48.635 --> 00:23:50.178
I wanna play a long time."

00:23:50.178 --> 00:23:51.555
- Cuz you spent your whole childhood

00:23:51.555 --> 00:23:52.472
preppin' for this career.

00:23:52.472 --> 00:23:53.348
- Yeah, man.

00:23:53.348 --> 00:23:55.559
And, you know, I got a Hurricane that with

00:23:55.559 --> 00:23:58.061
me in Ray Lewis, you know? And, um, I

00:23:58.061 --> 00:23:59.771
already knew a bunch of stuff about work

00:23:59.771 --> 00:24:01.690
ethic, workin' out and stuff, man.

00:24:01.690 --> 00:24:04.025
But Ray had a chef, you know? He was

00:24:04.025 --> 00:24:06.194
livin' a certain way. Ray had a mansion in

00:24:06.194 --> 00:24:07.863
Baltimore, you know, so.

00:24:08.363 --> 00:24:09.030
It wasn't so much as

00:24:09.030 --> 00:24:10.907
like, "Lemme get the mansion and everything."

00:24:10.907 --> 00:24:13.452
But the things that, at least I can say,

00:24:14.286 --> 00:24:15.620
if I wanted, I can get it.

00:24:16.121 --> 00:24:16.705
You know what I'm saying?

00:24:16.705 --> 00:24:17.622
Cuz there's a difference between a

00:24:17.622 --> 00:24:18.665
want and a need.
- Absolutely.

00:24:18.665 --> 00:24:19.708
- You know what I'm sayin'?

00:24:19.708 --> 00:24:21.751
And I didn't want it.

00:24:21.751 --> 00:24:23.336
I didn't need it, you know?

00:24:23.336 --> 00:24:26.298
But if I can say now, where I'm at now,

00:24:26.298 --> 00:24:28.008
shit, if I want it, I can go buy it.

00:24:28.383 --> 00:24:30.802
But at the same time, you gotta have the upkeep.

00:24:31.261 --> 00:24:33.638
So I was like, "Man, you gotta eat right.

00:24:33.638 --> 00:24:35.682
You gotta work out. You gotta train."

00:24:35.682 --> 00:24:38.894
And, quite frankly, I did enough, um,

00:24:38.894 --> 00:24:41.938
havin' enough good time here in Miami in

00:24:41.938 --> 00:24:45.484
college to where I can put the partyin' down.

00:24:45.484 --> 00:24:46.902
And that's what I did.

00:24:46.902 --> 00:24:49.196
You know, me and Bryant McKinnie used to go

00:24:49.196 --> 00:24:50.447
out all the time.

00:24:50.447 --> 00:24:54.242
- He was also in Baltimore too.
- Man, Bryant McKinnie lived right

00:24:54.242 --> 00:24:56.953
directly across from me in the condos in Brickell.

00:24:58.121 --> 00:24:59.789
And he know I'm not lyin'.

00:25:00.290 --> 00:25:02.083
He used to have this party bus.

00:25:02.083 --> 00:25:04.044
He used to pick up everybody. "Man, come

00:25:04.044 --> 00:25:05.712
on, Reed. Come on downstairs."

00:25:05.712 --> 00:25:07.339
I'm like, "Whatcha talkin' about man?"

00:25:07.339 --> 00:25:09.716
Like, "We goin' out." I look off the balcony.

00:25:10.550 --> 00:25:12.677
"Man, I'm not gettin' on that bus!"

00:25:14.262 --> 00:25:14.804
You know?

00:25:14.804 --> 00:25:16.264
But, um, yeah, man. I put it down.

00:25:16.264 --> 00:25:18.350
He's like, "Reed, you changed. You changed, Reed."

00:25:18.350 --> 00:25:19.059
And I did, man.

00:25:19.059 --> 00:25:20.810
I mean, I was—
- When was he sayin' you

00:25:20.810 --> 00:25:22.020
changed? When did people notice that?

00:25:22.020 --> 00:25:23.897
- I mean, I was reading the Word more,

00:25:23.897 --> 00:25:26.608
you know, and, and, and just finding

00:25:26.608 --> 00:25:29.819
myself, you know? I was finding myself.
- How old were you?

00:25:29.819 --> 00:25:33.073
- I was 23, you know, and killin' that old guy

00:25:33.073 --> 00:25:35.075
that, that, that was—I felt like was

00:25:35.075 --> 00:25:36.284
draggin' me down.

00:25:36.284 --> 00:25:36.993
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:25:36.993 --> 00:25:39.079
That could be people, it could be yourself.

00:25:39.454 --> 00:25:41.581
You know, if you feel like you've been

00:25:41.581 --> 00:25:44.876
dragged down, man, you gotta change some

00:25:44.876 --> 00:25:46.836
things. You know, as, as me and my boys

00:25:46.836 --> 00:25:49.339
say, you gotta take inventory on yourself

00:25:49.339 --> 00:25:50.507
and your surroundings, the people you

00:25:50.507 --> 00:25:51.466
surround yourself with.

00:25:51.800 --> 00:25:52.342
- That's real.

00:25:53.301 --> 00:25:53.969
That's your shot.

00:25:53.969 --> 00:25:54.594
I missed it

00:25:54.594 --> 00:25:55.428
while you were talkin' about the South

00:25:55.428 --> 00:25:56.930
Beach story. I couldn't focus.

00:25:56.930 --> 00:25:58.014
I couldn't get locked in.

00:25:58.014 --> 00:25:59.224
But you gave me one already.

00:25:59.224 --> 00:25:59.933
You gave us a donation.

00:26:03.645 --> 00:26:05.397
So on top of the Miami Hurricanes, do you

00:26:05.397 --> 00:26:06.773
always wear 'em on a rope like that?

00:26:06.773 --> 00:26:07.190
- Mhm.
- The chains?

00:26:07.816 --> 00:26:10.110
I see you got the, the Ravens chain—uh, ring on too.

00:26:10.110 --> 00:26:11.903
- Yeah, this some—I feel like this is a

00:26:11.903 --> 00:26:13.613
Miami thing cuz the first person I saw

00:26:13.613 --> 00:26:14.948
do it was Trick Daddy.

00:26:14.948 --> 00:26:15.365
- Oh, yeah?

00:26:15.740 --> 00:26:16.116
- Yeah.

00:26:16.116 --> 00:26:17.617
- When'd you see him do it?
- Man, this is

00:26:17.617 --> 00:26:19.286
back when I used to be runnin' with Trick.

00:26:19.286 --> 00:26:20.328
Trick took care of us.

00:26:20.328 --> 00:26:21.830
Took care of my family,

00:26:21.830 --> 00:26:23.331
you know, at times when I was here in

00:26:23.331 --> 00:26:26.334
Miami, you know. And, um, Trick, tend to

00:26:26.334 --> 00:26:28.211
get a bad rap, man, but he's a really good

00:26:28.211 --> 00:26:29.045
dude, man.

00:26:29.045 --> 00:26:31.464
And he truly do cares about the kids, you know?

00:26:31.464 --> 00:26:33.466
And, um, he loved Miami.

00:26:34.092 --> 00:26:35.969
And, uh, when I was here, man, I used to

00:26:35.969 --> 00:26:38.847
always be around him and his homeboy Twenty Two

00:26:38.847 --> 00:26:40.932
and just, just hangin' around.

00:26:40.932 --> 00:26:42.684
And, um, if somethin' was 'bout to happen,

00:26:42.684 --> 00:26:43.310
he'd let me know,

00:26:43.310 --> 00:26:45.145
"Reed, you need to get up outta here.

00:26:45.145 --> 00:26:46.229
Get your family. Let's go."

00:26:46.855 --> 00:26:47.188
You know?

00:26:47.188 --> 00:26:48.940
But, um, he was the first person I

00:26:48.940 --> 00:26:50.609
actually saw do this.

00:26:50.609 --> 00:26:53.153
And, um, I just kinda take it from there.

00:26:53.153 --> 00:26:55.405
Man, he was—I always called him Unc. I'll call him

00:26:55.405 --> 00:26:56.156
Uncle Trick.

00:26:56.156 --> 00:26:58.158
You know, I haven't seen him in a while,

00:26:58.158 --> 00:27:00.910
but yeah, man, he, he was a big part.

00:27:00.910 --> 00:27:04.623
He was a big part of, um, growth, for

00:27:04.623 --> 00:27:06.249
me in college.

00:27:06.249 --> 00:27:09.044
- So you got the 2012 Super Bowl ring

00:27:09.044 --> 00:27:10.462
wrapped around your neck as well.

00:27:10.462 --> 00:27:11.504
- No, no, no.

00:27:11.504 --> 00:27:12.881
This is my Hall of Fame ring.

00:27:12.881 --> 00:27:13.673
- Oh, your Hall of Fame ring.

00:27:13.673 --> 00:27:14.341
Oh, OK.
- Yeah. This is my Hall of Fame ring.

00:27:14.341 --> 00:27:16.092
- That joint big too.

00:27:16.092 --> 00:27:16.509
- Yeah,

00:27:16.509 --> 00:27:17.010
it's the biggest.

00:27:17.010 --> 00:27:17.761
I can't even fit it.

00:27:17.761 --> 00:27:19.220
I don't think nobody in here can put this on

00:27:19.220 --> 00:27:20.096
their finger.

00:27:20.096 --> 00:27:21.222
- Where's your Super Bowl ring at?

00:27:21.222 --> 00:27:23.558
- The Super Bowl ring is actually back at the hotel.

00:27:23.558 --> 00:27:26.269
Um, the diamond off the first Super Bowl

00:27:26.269 --> 00:27:28.146
came off, so—
- Oh, we gotta get that fixed.

00:27:28.146 --> 00:27:28.438
- Yeah.

00:27:28.438 --> 00:27:29.481
But I wasn't a part of that one

00:27:29.481 --> 00:27:33.193
anyway, so...I'm not trippin'.

00:27:34.486 --> 00:27:36.363
- What do you take away from that year, though?

00:27:36.363 --> 00:27:39.574
Cuz you guys, during that season,

00:27:40.158 --> 00:27:41.493
you got your ass whipped by the Texans.

00:27:41.493 --> 00:27:41.868
- Yeah.

00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:43.370
- There was a bye week that happened.

00:27:43.370 --> 00:27:45.413
- Then afterwards, what happened during

00:27:45.413 --> 00:27:50.001
practice that week?
- Man, we, we had a

00:27:50.001 --> 00:27:51.544
meeting with the coach.

00:27:51.544 --> 00:27:56.174
We had a meeting with Coach Harbaugh, um,

00:27:56.174 --> 00:27:59.052
before the team meeting. Team meeting's at 8:30.

00:27:59.052 --> 00:28:00.387
We had an 8:00 meeting with the

00:28:00.387 --> 00:28:01.680
head coach in his office.

00:28:03.056 --> 00:28:05.100
He's like, "Guys, I'm thinkin' about

00:28:05.100 --> 00:28:06.893
puttin' y'all in pads for the bye week.

00:28:06.893 --> 00:28:09.229
We were like, "Coach, no way.

00:28:09.229 --> 00:28:12.899
We're not doin' pads, man, in the bye week."

00:28:12.899 --> 00:28:14.859
You know, like, we're an older team. We don't need that.

00:28:14.859 --> 00:28:15.235
- Yeah.

00:28:15.235 --> 00:28:15.485
- Right?

00:28:15.485 --> 00:28:17.487
And he was like—long story short, he was

00:28:17.487 --> 00:28:19.989
like, "All right, I'm not gonna do it.

00:28:19.989 --> 00:28:21.449
We're not gonna put you guys in pads."

00:28:21.449 --> 00:28:23.410
Man, we walk right downstairs, and Coach was

00:28:23.410 --> 00:28:25.495
like, "All right, we're gonna put you

00:28:25.495 --> 00:28:26.454
guys in full pads."

00:28:26.454 --> 00:28:27.580
We was like, "No!"

00:28:28.081 --> 00:28:29.999
And so I raised my hand. Like, "Coach, come on, man.

00:28:29.999 --> 00:28:32.711
You just said, you know, you wasn't gonna do that.

00:28:32.711 --> 00:28:33.795
You can't go back on your word."

00:28:33.795 --> 00:28:34.462
And he was like, "Yeah, Ed.

00:28:34.462 --> 00:28:36.589
I changed my mind walkin' down the stairs."

00:28:36.589 --> 00:28:37.549
I'm like, "No.

00:28:38.007 --> 00:28:40.093
Nah, man, you can't, you can't do that to

00:28:40.093 --> 00:28:40.844
us, man."

00:28:40.844 --> 00:28:41.720
You know?

00:28:41.720 --> 00:28:44.347
So that led to Bernard Pollard gettin' up,

00:28:45.014 --> 00:28:48.601
standin' up, and then it got heated. At

00:28:48.601 --> 00:28:52.105
some point, Bernard is yellin' at Coach Harbaugh.

00:28:52.105 --> 00:28:53.481
And it was like, "Tell him, Ed!"

00:28:53.481 --> 00:28:55.608
I'm like, "Whoa, I already said what I had to say!"

00:28:57.861 --> 00:28:59.571
You know, he threw me back in that fire,

00:28:59.571 --> 00:29:02.407
man, and I, you know, I chimed in, but it

00:29:02.407 --> 00:29:04.200
really, it really resonated when Haloti

00:29:04.200 --> 00:29:05.452
Ngata—who don't say nothin'

00:29:05.452 --> 00:29:06.494
he's a gentle giant—

00:29:07.036 --> 00:29:09.914
um, is my guy, and everybody know Haloti

00:29:09.914 --> 00:29:10.749
don't say much.

00:29:10.749 --> 00:29:12.500
Haloti was like, "Coach, you did tell us that."

00:29:13.084 --> 00:29:14.919
And when Haloti said it, the team backed

00:29:14.919 --> 00:29:19.758
it. And that right there brought us together.

00:29:19.758 --> 00:29:20.425
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:29:20.425 --> 00:29:22.594
Cuz that was, that was, that was goin'

00:29:22.594 --> 00:29:23.970
into the playoffs, man.

00:29:23.970 --> 00:29:26.264
We had to go to Denver. We had to go to

00:29:26.264 --> 00:29:27.307
New England.

00:29:27.307 --> 00:29:28.099
You know what I'm sayin', so—

00:29:28.099 --> 00:29:29.350
- It was a tough road.
- Yeah, man.

00:29:29.350 --> 00:29:32.228
And we was already on that, as I can use

00:29:32.228 --> 00:29:34.898
now, on that Mamba Mentality

00:29:34.898 --> 00:29:37.692
of every day, workin' hard, man. Anquan can

00:29:37.692 --> 00:29:39.778
tell you. Ray can tell you. Sizzle can

00:29:39.778 --> 00:29:41.154
tell you. Every day,

00:29:41.154 --> 00:29:43.698
we did the same stuff all the way up until

00:29:43.698 --> 00:29:45.116
the Super Bowl.

00:29:45.116 --> 00:29:45.992
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:29:45.992 --> 00:29:48.828
We had some bumps in there, some speed

00:29:48.828 --> 00:29:50.205
bumps that you gotta go over.

00:29:50.246 --> 00:29:50.914
That's natural.

00:29:50.914 --> 00:29:52.248
That's life, you know?

00:29:52.248 --> 00:29:55.418
But it was, it was, it was somethin' that

00:29:55.418 --> 00:29:56.753
really brought us together.

00:29:56.753 --> 00:29:57.670
- What are you always gonna take away

00:29:57.670 --> 00:29:58.505
from that season?

00:29:59.547 --> 00:29:59.964
- Man,

00:29:59.964 --> 00:30:00.840
the memories, man.

00:30:00.840 --> 00:30:02.967
Just bein' with my boys at practice.

00:30:02.967 --> 00:30:04.803
You know, the, the dedication that they

00:30:04.803 --> 00:30:07.180
put into it, the discipline, you know?

00:30:07.180 --> 00:30:07.889
- Practice is, practice is

00:30:07.889 --> 00:30:09.224
one of those memories that are always

00:30:09.224 --> 00:30:09.974
gonna pop up for you?

00:30:09.974 --> 00:30:10.600
- Oh, yeah, man.

00:30:10.600 --> 00:30:11.601
A locker room.

00:30:11.601 --> 00:30:13.937
You know, we, we had a cornhole

00:30:13.937 --> 00:30:15.980
tournament that year.
- Who won?

00:30:15.980 --> 00:30:19.275
- I think Sam Koch won. Sam Koch won.
- Oh, Sam Koch?

00:30:19.275 --> 00:30:21.486
- Yeah, the um—of course, they got all the time in the

00:30:21.486 --> 00:30:23.571
world! Our kickers, snappers and punters.

00:30:23.571 --> 00:30:24.155
They in there.

00:30:24.155 --> 00:30:25.281
You walk in there, they throwin'

00:30:25.281 --> 00:30:26.324
cornhole, you know?

00:30:26.324 --> 00:30:27.492
So, that was that, man.

00:30:27.492 --> 00:30:28.868
That was something that—but that was just

00:30:28.868 --> 00:30:30.119
bringin' us together.

00:30:30.119 --> 00:30:32.163
Um, I remember we—I used to, I used to

00:30:32.163 --> 00:30:33.998
tell the guys, "Don't eat at the facility

00:30:33.998 --> 00:30:36.209
after practice." And we used to all go to

00:30:36.209 --> 00:30:39.337
Yuki Sushi and just have drinks and just

00:30:39.337 --> 00:30:40.588
sit in there, man. It's gettin',

00:30:40.588 --> 00:30:42.549
gettin' to know your teammates away from

00:30:42.549 --> 00:30:45.343
the football field, you know what I'm sayin'?
- Why was that important?

00:30:45.343 --> 00:30:47.971
- That's very important to know your coworkers, who you

00:30:47.971 --> 00:30:48.680
workin' with, man.

00:30:48.680 --> 00:30:50.974
How they are, you know what I'm sayin'?

00:30:50.974 --> 00:30:53.977
How are they gonna handle things away

00:30:53.977 --> 00:30:56.354
from the business? That tells you a lot.

00:30:56.354 --> 00:30:59.649
If you—so, you might see, I did this—I did a, um,

00:30:59.649 --> 00:31:03.111
sitdown with Joe Buck, and I put, I put my

00:31:03.111 --> 00:31:05.530
teammates on blast, but I had already did

00:31:05.530 --> 00:31:08.032
it in the meeting, where I told 'em, like,

00:31:08.032 --> 00:31:11.578
"Bro, we got volunteer firemen around here,

00:31:11.578 --> 00:31:13.705
and y'all grown men throwin' your trash

00:31:13.705 --> 00:31:15.498
right in front of the garbage can."

00:31:15.498 --> 00:31:17.458
They would miss it and leave it there

00:31:17.458 --> 00:31:18.877
cuz they know somebody was gonna

00:31:18.877 --> 00:31:20.962
take care of it, you know, or leave their

00:31:20.962 --> 00:31:21.963
towel in the shower.

00:31:21.963 --> 00:31:23.590
Man, you can imagine bein' in the locker

00:31:23.590 --> 00:31:26.050
room, going into the, in the shower,

00:31:26.050 --> 00:31:29.637
and dirty towels are everywhere on the damn

00:31:29.637 --> 00:31:31.598
knob that you gotta turn the water on.

00:31:31.598 --> 00:31:32.974
- What'd you take that as a sign of when you

00:31:32.974 --> 00:31:33.808
saw that?

00:31:33.808 --> 00:31:35.476
- Disrespect.

00:31:35.476 --> 00:31:38.396
It's disgusting, and that's stuff kids do.

00:31:38.980 --> 00:31:40.648
I'm correcting my son on this,

00:31:40.648 --> 00:31:41.399
and y'all doin' it?

00:31:41.399 --> 00:31:42.483
No way, man.

00:31:42.483 --> 00:31:44.402
This is your house away from your house,

00:31:44.402 --> 00:31:46.154
your home away from home.

00:31:46.154 --> 00:31:46.696
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:31:46.696 --> 00:31:49.574
When I lost my brother in 2011, those were

00:31:49.574 --> 00:31:52.368
my family members to hold me together.

00:31:52.368 --> 00:31:53.745
So this is our home.

00:31:53.745 --> 00:31:55.747
But more than, more than anything, respect

00:31:55.747 --> 00:31:56.581
those firemen, dude.

00:31:56.581 --> 00:31:58.750
They're grown men, dude.

00:31:58.750 --> 00:32:00.627
They shouldn't have to be pickin' up after you.

00:32:01.044 --> 00:32:02.754
They're not your maids.

00:32:02.754 --> 00:32:03.504
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:32:03.504 --> 00:32:05.632
And if we wanna win a championship—me

00:32:05.632 --> 00:32:08.009
and Ray had this conversation my rookie year.

00:32:08.009 --> 00:32:09.344
He was just like, "Bro, we gotta do the

00:32:09.344 --> 00:32:10.303
little things, man."

00:32:11.137 --> 00:32:12.430
Cuz little things, if you don't do the

00:32:12.430 --> 00:32:14.307
lil' things, it'll turn into big stuff.

00:32:15.475 --> 00:32:17.977
- There's always alpha males and egos in the locker room.

00:32:19.395 --> 00:32:20.980
You and Ray Lewis

00:32:20.980 --> 00:32:22.607
are two alpha males that I feel like, in

00:32:22.607 --> 00:32:23.983
terms of a leadership perspective, kind of

00:32:23.983 --> 00:32:25.151
approach things differently.

00:32:25.151 --> 00:32:25.443
- Yeah.

00:32:25.443 --> 00:32:27.987
- But it seemed like you always were able to mesh.

00:32:27.987 --> 00:32:28.237
- Yeah.

00:32:28.237 --> 00:32:28.738
- Why?

00:32:29.322 --> 00:32:32.408
- Cuz, man, look, I didn't care.

00:32:32.408 --> 00:32:34.744
I didn't care who got the glory, man.

00:32:34.744 --> 00:32:37.330
I come from Miami, where we had many stars.

00:32:37.330 --> 00:32:38.957
We had a lotta first-rounders.

00:32:39.374 --> 00:32:41.501
I come from Miami, where we were pushin'

00:32:41.501 --> 00:32:42.919
each other to be great.

00:32:43.544 --> 00:32:45.254
That's what I come from around, and that's

00:32:45.254 --> 00:32:46.422
what it's about.

00:32:46.422 --> 00:32:49.467
There's enough out here for everybody to have.

00:32:49.467 --> 00:32:52.178
They print money all the time.

00:32:52.178 --> 00:32:53.805
They print money as we speak.

00:32:53.805 --> 00:32:56.224
That's enough for everybody.

00:32:56.224 --> 00:32:58.434
So all we gotta do is just encourage each other.

00:32:58.434 --> 00:33:01.104
If we work together, if we work together,

00:33:01.104 --> 00:33:03.523
we gonna win. And everybody will have

00:33:03.523 --> 00:33:07.276
an opportunity to do something, to be in the league.

00:33:07.276 --> 00:33:09.320
My whole senior class had a opportunity

00:33:09.320 --> 00:33:12.115
to go to the NFL and went to a team.

00:33:12.115 --> 00:33:14.117
What you do with that opportunity's on yours.

00:33:14.701 --> 00:33:17.120
I was gonna put mine in a chokehold, as

00:33:17.120 --> 00:33:18.913
D.C. Young Fly would say.

00:33:18.913 --> 00:33:21.249
Shout-out to 85 South. Them my boys.

00:33:21.708 --> 00:33:23.918
But, real stuff, when you get that

00:33:23.918 --> 00:33:25.712
opportunity, man, you gotta put it in a

00:33:25.712 --> 00:33:28.089
chokehold. Cuz if you don't, somebody

00:33:28.089 --> 00:33:29.882
else, especially at the University of

00:33:29.882 --> 00:33:32.176
Miami, was right behind you waitin' for

00:33:32.176 --> 00:33:33.511
the opportunity.
- As you shared,

00:33:33.511 --> 00:33:35.888
Sean Taylor was behind you.
- Real stuff!

00:33:35.888 --> 00:33:36.639
And I wasn't havin' it.

00:33:36.639 --> 00:33:37.223
It wasn't happenin'.

00:33:37.849 --> 00:33:38.641
It wasn't happenin'.

00:33:39.017 --> 00:33:39.517
No.

00:33:39.517 --> 00:33:41.144
I'm playin' with three broken legs.

00:33:41.436 --> 00:33:43.312
- How beneficial was it for you to have Ray

00:33:43.312 --> 00:33:45.440
Lewis at the start of your career in the

00:33:45.440 --> 00:33:47.275
NFL to kinda show the leadership

00:33:47.275 --> 00:33:49.360
and guidance of how to adjust to the

00:33:49.360 --> 00:33:50.486
professional level?

00:33:50.486 --> 00:33:52.739
- Man, it was key to have Ray, Peter Boulware,

00:33:52.739 --> 00:33:56.576
Derrick Mason, Matt Stover.

00:33:56.576 --> 00:33:58.286
It was a lotta leaders, man.

00:33:58.286 --> 00:34:00.038
It was not just one leader, you know?

00:34:00.038 --> 00:34:01.414
I know that people that always saw me

00:34:01.414 --> 00:34:04.208
with Ray, and we were, we were like peas in a pod cuz

00:34:04.208 --> 00:34:04.876
we was Hurricanes.

00:34:04.876 --> 00:34:07.837
We were always together, but it was Monte

00:34:07.837 --> 00:34:09.297
Sanders who was with us too,

00:34:09.297 --> 00:34:10.465
our trainer.

00:34:10.465 --> 00:34:11.090
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:34:11.090 --> 00:34:12.717
Rohan Marley that was with us.

00:34:12.717 --> 00:34:15.136
We trained in Jamaica together, so it wasn't

00:34:15.136 --> 00:34:16.387
just Ray, man.

00:34:16.387 --> 00:34:17.346
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:34:17.346 --> 00:34:20.475
It was many people, cuz not every time

00:34:20.475 --> 00:34:22.602
me and Ray saw eye-to-eye, you know what

00:34:22.602 --> 00:34:23.394
I'm sayin'?

00:34:23.394 --> 00:34:24.395
But I didn't care.

00:34:24.395 --> 00:34:24.854
I don't care.

00:34:24.854 --> 00:34:26.355
I didn't care who get the glory, man.

00:34:26.355 --> 00:34:28.149
I can care less about that.

00:34:28.149 --> 00:34:29.984
Like I said, there's enough for everybody.

00:34:29.984 --> 00:34:31.611
I'mmma take my little bit, have me a

00:34:31.611 --> 00:34:33.988
cigar and keep it movin'.

00:34:35.073 --> 00:34:36.699
- Is it my shot? I think it's your shot.

00:34:36.699 --> 00:34:37.325
- It's your shot.
- Oh, it's my shot? OK.

00:34:37.325 --> 00:34:38.076
- It's your shot.

00:34:38.076 --> 00:34:38.743
- I gotta, I gotta make one, bro.

00:34:38.743 --> 00:34:40.536
- If it's not your shot, I'll give it to you

00:34:40.536 --> 00:34:41.412
cuz you need some help.

00:34:42.622 --> 00:34:43.790
- Lemme get this one right here.

00:34:43.790 --> 00:34:45.458
- You got a lotta kids in the street.

00:34:47.585 --> 00:34:48.461
- You feelin' charitable.

00:34:49.712 --> 00:34:51.214
How was it like for you

00:34:53.466 --> 00:34:54.967
to win a Super Bowl? We had, we had Jacoby

00:34:54.967 --> 00:34:57.428
Jones on Season 1, also a New Orleans native.
- Yeah.

00:34:57.428 --> 00:34:58.137
- That, that—I loved

00:34:58.137 --> 00:34:58.679
talkin' to him.

00:34:58.679 --> 00:35:01.599
- That's my boy.
- Season 1, we talked to him about

00:35:01.599 --> 00:35:02.683
being able to win in New Orleans.

00:35:02.683 --> 00:35:04.018
- Yeah.
- Winnin' the Super Bowl in New Orleans.

00:35:04.018 --> 00:35:04.352
- Yeah.

00:35:04.352 --> 00:35:05.561
I wanna ask you after I take this shot

00:35:05.561 --> 00:35:07.897
real quick, you know what I'm sayin'?
- Yeah, I know. I'm tryna get this out

00:35:07.897 --> 00:35:08.397
your way, man.

00:35:08.397 --> 00:35:09.398
I'm tryna help you out.

00:35:10.650 --> 00:35:12.568
- What was it like for you as a New Orleans native—

00:35:12.568 --> 00:35:17.073
- Yeah, I'm from New Orleans. I'm from that city.
- To win the Super Bowl?

00:35:17.073 --> 00:35:21.077
- So, for, for us to go back home, it was everything

00:35:21.077 --> 00:35:22.078
to me, man.

00:35:23.037 --> 00:35:25.373
So the week we're going to New Orleans for

00:35:25.373 --> 00:35:28.459
the Super Bowl, I had to give a speech to

00:35:28.459 --> 00:35:30.795
the guys about not eatin' a lotta food.

00:35:31.337 --> 00:35:33.965
Cuz I'm like, "Dude, come Wednesday, Thursday,

00:35:33.965 --> 00:35:35.758
don't be eatin' all this food, man.

00:35:35.758 --> 00:35:37.468
You're gonna be heavy and sluggish."

00:35:37.468 --> 00:35:38.678
- I wanna give you a spoiler alert.

00:35:38.678 --> 00:35:39.428
Jacoby Jones

00:35:39.428 --> 00:35:41.139
did not listen. His momma was cookin' gumbo

00:35:41.139 --> 00:35:41.639
and everything.

00:35:41.639 --> 00:35:43.141
- My mom cooked too for the guys.

00:35:43.141 --> 00:35:45.810
Nah, he listened. Everybody listened. Jacoby shoulda got MVP.

00:35:45.810 --> 00:35:47.353
Had two touchdowns.

00:35:47.353 --> 00:35:48.062
Kickoff return.

00:35:48.062 --> 00:35:48.771
Come on, man.

00:35:48.771 --> 00:35:49.355
- That's real.

00:35:49.355 --> 00:35:49.730
- You know,

00:35:49.730 --> 00:35:52.567
so, I'm like, "Guys, be mindful, man,

00:35:52.567 --> 00:35:53.359
eatin' the food."

00:35:53.359 --> 00:35:54.110
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:35:54.110 --> 00:35:56.112
I brought crawfish and everything to the

00:35:56.112 --> 00:35:59.824
hotels for the boys, you know, but we still

00:35:59.824 --> 00:36:02.660
on a business trip, so treat it as such,

00:36:02.660 --> 00:36:03.035
you know?

00:36:03.035 --> 00:36:04.954
And plus, man, I'm in my backyard.

00:36:04.954 --> 00:36:06.956
I'm not losin' in my backyard.

00:36:06.956 --> 00:36:07.582
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:36:07.582 --> 00:36:11.335
I ate oysters every day, so...everybody

00:36:11.335 --> 00:36:12.587
know what oysters will do for you.

00:36:14.172 --> 00:36:17.175
- [Laughing]
- Come pregame,

00:36:17.175 --> 00:36:18.509
I was jacked up!

00:36:19.802 --> 00:36:20.428
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:36:20.428 --> 00:36:22.555
I actually hurt myself in the first quarter.

00:36:22.555 --> 00:36:23.890
I hurt both of my MCLs.

00:36:23.890 --> 00:36:24.932
I sprained both of them.
- Sprained 'em both,

00:36:24.932 --> 00:36:25.224
yeah.

00:36:25.224 --> 00:36:25.850
- Yeah, man.

00:36:25.850 --> 00:36:29.103
So, but, but to, to do it at home, man, it

00:36:29.103 --> 00:36:30.646
was a true ble—I can't really even

00:36:30.646 --> 00:36:33.816
describe winning a championship at home.

00:36:33.816 --> 00:36:34.942
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:36:34.942 --> 00:36:37.737
We was the last team to play in the

00:36:37.737 --> 00:36:43.826
Superdome before Katrina hit, right?

00:36:43.826 --> 00:36:45.369
We were the first team to play in the

00:36:45.369 --> 00:36:49.290
Super Bowl in New Orleans after Katrina.

00:36:49.290 --> 00:36:52.168
So for me, it's, it's a lot, man. Special, man.

00:36:52.168 --> 00:36:53.377
It's so much.

00:36:53.377 --> 00:36:54.921
You know, I look at Baltimore and New

00:36:54.921 --> 00:36:57.506
Orleans as twin cities because of the

00:36:57.506 --> 00:37:00.218
stuff that they experienced in the inner cities.

00:37:00.218 --> 00:37:02.428
- Economically, the way it's structured, yeah.
- Yeah, man. you watch "Treme,"

00:37:02.428 --> 00:37:05.181
you watch "The Wire"—
- Same, same—yeah.

00:37:05.181 --> 00:37:07.683
- You get a real good glimpse of how the cities are, 

00:37:07.683 --> 00:37:08.226
you know?

00:37:08.226 --> 00:37:11.562
So it was special to me, man.

00:37:11.562 --> 00:37:13.606
And now Baltimore, New Orleans people,

00:37:13.606 --> 00:37:15.608
when they get together, man, it's a blast.

00:37:15.608 --> 00:37:16.442
It's a party.

00:37:18.819 --> 00:37:19.570
- So that—was it—

00:37:19.570 --> 00:37:21.364
- Is it my shot?
- Yeah, it's your

00:37:21.364 --> 00:37:21.781
shot right now.

00:37:21.781 --> 00:37:22.615
I made two real quick.

00:37:22.615 --> 00:37:23.074
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:37:23.074 --> 00:37:28.246
I didn't wanna embarrass myself, so—that was a nice shot.

00:37:30.081 --> 00:37:30.706
Correct me if I'm wrong:

00:37:30.706 --> 00:37:33.793
That was your last game as a Raven.

00:37:33.793 --> 00:37:35.294
- That was my last game as a Raven,

00:37:35.294 --> 00:37:36.379
unfortunately, yes.

00:37:38.172 --> 00:37:39.131
- Did you know it was gonna end there?

00:37:40.299 --> 00:37:41.300
- I had a clue.

00:37:42.426 --> 00:37:43.928
I had a clue it was gonna end there

00:37:43.928 --> 00:37:46.430
because when Ray Lewis became a free

00:37:46.430 --> 00:37:49.141
agent, I was at the house with him, and

00:37:49.141 --> 00:37:54.188
they didn't offer him too much, so it's

00:37:54.188 --> 00:37:55.690
a business, man.

00:37:55.690 --> 00:37:57.400
I learned a lot from Steve Bisciotti,

00:37:57.400 --> 00:38:00.820
Ozzie Newsome, those guys, you know, and

00:38:01.654 --> 00:38:03.155
they taught me so much.

00:38:03.155 --> 00:38:04.532
Excuse me for turnin' my back to you

00:38:04.532 --> 00:38:06.867
guys, but they taught me so much about the

00:38:06.867 --> 00:38:09.829
business, so I wasn't surprised when I

00:38:09.829 --> 00:38:12.123
called, cuz I didn't have an agent.

00:38:12.123 --> 00:38:13.624
I was my own agent.

00:38:13.624 --> 00:38:15.793
I called and I'm like, "What's goin' on?

00:38:15.793 --> 00:38:16.752
What we doin'?"

00:38:16.752 --> 00:38:19.547
And they were like, "We don't have nothin' for you."

00:38:19.547 --> 00:38:20.756
I'm like, "Not even a dollar?"

00:38:20.756 --> 00:38:23.426
They didn't offer me a dollar. Not a dime.

00:38:24.635 --> 00:38:27.346
So I was like, "All right, my time is up."

00:38:27.346 --> 00:38:30.725
You know, my time is up. So I was all right with it.

00:38:30.725 --> 00:38:32.601
- I think I said in the Super Bowl, it was, it was
a MCL

00:38:32.601 --> 00:38:35.563
sprain? Was it a tear?
- I had a second-degree

00:38:35.563 --> 00:38:38.107
MCL sprain on my left leg and the first-

00:38:38.107 --> 00:38:39.483
degree on my right.

00:38:39.483 --> 00:38:41.819
So as much as I wanted to pick off three

00:38:41.819 --> 00:38:43.696
and four picks from Kaepernick, cuz I knew

00:38:43.696 --> 00:38:45.448
I had 'em, I couldn't move.
- Mhm.

00:38:46.073 --> 00:38:47.116
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:38:47.116 --> 00:38:49.243
And to be honest with you, that was—we

00:38:49.243 --> 00:38:52.246
played the Saints in the Superdome so many times.

00:38:52.705 --> 00:38:54.623
That was the only interception I ever had

00:38:54.623 --> 00:38:55.416
in the Superdome.

00:38:55.958 --> 00:38:56.417
- Wow.

00:38:56.417 --> 00:38:56.876
- Yeah, man.

00:38:56.876 --> 00:39:00.087
We partied one time down there, like, 2006. We

00:39:00.087 --> 00:39:02.465
was kickin' they butt, and the boys on the

00:39:02.465 --> 00:39:05.051
sideline dancin', doin' everything.

00:39:05.051 --> 00:39:07.094
Everybody from Louisiana—we had, like, five

00:39:07.094 --> 00:39:09.555
guys on the team from Louisiana.

00:39:09.555 --> 00:39:11.390
Everybody except for me caught an interception that

00:39:11.390 --> 00:39:13.642
game. But in the Super Bowl, when it

00:39:13.642 --> 00:39:16.187
counts, the hawk showed up.
- You stepped up. You stepped up.

00:39:16.187 --> 00:39:16.604
- Yes, sir.

00:39:16.604 --> 00:39:17.980
- So the Ravens have seen you comin' off the

00:39:17.980 --> 00:39:19.982
injury in the Superdo—in the Super Bowl.

00:39:20.566 --> 00:39:23.069
You're getting older. Your time's up there.

00:39:23.069 --> 00:39:24.945
You end up spending time with the Houston Texans.

00:39:24.945 --> 00:39:26.530
You reunited with Andre Johnson.

00:39:26.530 --> 00:39:27.406
- If I'd have stayed in Baltimore,

00:39:27.406 --> 00:39:28.657
I'd still be playin' today.

00:39:28.657 --> 00:39:29.408
- You think so?

00:39:29.408 --> 00:39:30.034
- I know so.

00:39:30.034 --> 00:39:30.493
- Why?

00:39:30.993 --> 00:39:33.454
- Because, man, I just had, I had everything

00:39:33.454 --> 00:39:34.747
goin' the right way.

00:39:34.747 --> 00:39:37.666
I had my doctor, training, everything.

00:39:38.584 --> 00:39:40.378
When I went to Houston, it took me back to

00:39:40.378 --> 00:39:42.963
the South, and it was so discouraging.

00:39:42.963 --> 00:39:46.342
If y'all remember, um, rest in peace to

00:39:46.342 --> 00:39:48.260
him, Bob McNair,

00:39:48.260 --> 00:39:50.137
but outta respect.

00:39:50.137 --> 00:39:53.015
Um, if y'all remember when we were goin'

00:39:53.015 --> 00:39:54.934
through certain things in the NFL, you

00:39:54.934 --> 00:39:56.435
remember he said, "You can't let the

00:39:56.435 --> 00:39:57.978
prisoners run the prison."

00:39:57.978 --> 00:39:59.355
He was the guy that say that.

00:39:59.355 --> 00:40:01.315
I saw that before.

00:40:01.315 --> 00:40:03.651
I saw that when I was in, when I was there.

00:40:04.026 --> 00:40:06.737
You know—
- The way he ran things, or—

00:40:06.737 --> 00:40:09.323
- How, how—the Houston way, so to say, that they

00:40:09.323 --> 00:40:10.991
called it. You know, and it's not the

00:40:10.991 --> 00:40:13.452
city, it's the organization, you know, at

00:40:13.452 --> 00:40:13.994
the time.

00:40:13.994 --> 00:40:16.038
And they had that old-school mentality,

00:40:16.038 --> 00:40:18.207
man. You had coaches talkin' reckless to guys.

00:40:18.833 --> 00:40:19.166
You know?

00:40:19.166 --> 00:40:21.627
And I'm like, as a grown man, how do you

00:40:21.627 --> 00:40:23.003
let that happen?

00:40:23.003 --> 00:40:25.089
And plus, as a coach, you're not gonna

00:40:25.089 --> 00:40:27.967
get everything out of that man that you

00:40:27.967 --> 00:40:30.177
want because you talkin' to him reckless.

00:40:30.177 --> 00:40:32.096
I was surrounded by a bunch of guys just

00:40:32.096 --> 00:40:33.264
comin' to work.

00:40:33.264 --> 00:40:34.140
It was a job.

00:40:34.140 --> 00:40:35.558
It wasn't football anymore.

00:40:35.933 --> 00:40:37.768
And for me, that was, like, draining.

00:40:38.269 --> 00:40:39.687
That was very draining, man.

00:40:39.687 --> 00:40:42.064
And, "You actually brought me in, recruited

00:40:42.064 --> 00:40:43.732
me and asked me to tell you the things

00:40:43.732 --> 00:40:44.525
that I'm tellin' you.

00:40:45.359 --> 00:40:47.611
And when I started sayin' that stuff,

00:40:48.070 --> 00:40:49.822
they shrugged me off, you know? It was like,

00:40:49.822 --> 00:40:52.450
"Eh, that's, that's not how we do it."

00:40:52.450 --> 00:40:54.118
So I was lied to.

00:40:54.702 --> 00:40:56.495
I'm too old to be lied to, and I'm too old

00:40:56.495 --> 00:40:58.664
to play these games with people who, you

00:40:58.664 --> 00:41:02.626
know, say you gotta play the game to get higher.

00:41:02.626 --> 00:41:03.419
I'm not playin' the game.

00:41:03.419 --> 00:41:04.086
I ain't got time for that.

00:41:04.086 --> 00:41:05.546
I'll go do something else.

00:41:05.546 --> 00:41:06.464
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:41:06.464 --> 00:41:08.466
I'll go work back at Blockbuster.

00:41:09.383 --> 00:41:10.926
I worked at Blockbuster when I was here in

00:41:10.926 --> 00:41:13.012
college, so, kids, you could have a job.

00:41:13.762 --> 00:41:15.514
- So you went from there. Then you went to the Jets.

00:41:16.265 --> 00:41:17.975
And then how'd you know it was time to

00:41:17.975 --> 00:41:18.684
hang it up at that point?

00:41:18.684 --> 00:41:20.060
Cuz you're sharin' right now that you

00:41:20.060 --> 00:41:22.605
feel like you could still be playing.
- Only reason, only reason I retired

00:41:22.605 --> 00:41:25.107
was to bring some joy to Baltimore.

00:41:25.733 --> 00:41:28.110
Freddie Gray had just got killed, man.

00:41:29.361 --> 00:41:31.280
And I was sittin' in my house in Georgia.

00:41:31.280 --> 00:41:32.156
It was like, "You know what?

00:41:32.907 --> 00:41:33.407
I'm done."

00:41:34.742 --> 00:41:36.535
You know, I let these young folk have it.

00:41:36.869 --> 00:41:38.454
Let one of these young kids make the

00:41:38.454 --> 00:41:39.914
roster and let him make some money.

00:41:39.914 --> 00:41:40.873
Man, I'm good.

00:41:40.873 --> 00:41:42.625
I took care of my finances, still take

00:41:42.625 --> 00:41:43.375
care of my finances.

00:41:43.375 --> 00:41:45.294
So I wasn't, I wasn't one of those old

00:41:45.294 --> 00:41:48.047
guys, like, need to stay playin', you know?

00:41:48.088 --> 00:41:51.050
So I was like, "Bring some joy back home."

00:41:51.050 --> 00:41:54.261
And I started buildin' my foundation up in

00:41:54.261 --> 00:41:55.429
Baltimore even more.

00:41:55.429 --> 00:41:57.264
- Why'd you feel it was on you to bring

00:41:57.264 --> 00:41:58.933
the city joy through your retirement

00:41:58.933 --> 00:42:00.809
during a really rough time for that city?

00:42:00.809 --> 00:42:02.853
- Cuz, I mean, I was in that light, you

00:42:02.853 --> 00:42:04.396
know? It's kinda like Kobe, man. You're

00:42:04.396 --> 00:42:05.189
in that light.

00:42:05.189 --> 00:42:06.565
What can you do,

00:42:06.565 --> 00:42:09.860
you know? How, how can—cuz all we here to

00:42:09.860 --> 00:42:11.695
do is leave this place a better place than

00:42:11.695 --> 00:42:12.780
we got it when we came.

00:42:12.780 --> 00:42:13.906
- Preach.
- You know?

00:42:13.906 --> 00:42:16.742
So as for me and my advisor Glenn, my

00:42:16.742 --> 00:42:18.619
director of my foundation, started talkin'

00:42:18.619 --> 00:42:20.663
about doin' more stuff in the community.

00:42:20.663 --> 00:42:23.374
Man, we went—I went from my first year

00:42:23.374 --> 00:42:25.709
startin' my foundation, feedin' 30 people

00:42:25.709 --> 00:42:28.379
with my financial advisor, Brad Davis,

00:42:28.379 --> 00:42:29.547
feedin' 30 people.

00:42:29.547 --> 00:42:32.550
We used to go to the store ourselves, pick

00:42:32.550 --> 00:42:34.301
up the bags, pick up

00:42:34.301 --> 00:42:36.387
the turkeys, the fixings and everything.

00:42:36.387 --> 00:42:39.223
Excuse me, and bag it ourselves.

00:42:39.223 --> 00:42:41.183
Bring it to Booker T. Washington.

00:42:41.183 --> 00:42:43.936
We went from 30 to now 20 years later,

00:42:43.936 --> 00:42:45.813
feedin' like, 5,000.
- Dope.

00:42:45.854 --> 00:42:49.149
- You know, so that's what it's about.
- That's incredible, man.

00:42:49.149 --> 00:42:53.362
That's incredible.
- You know, that, that's what it's about.

00:42:53.362 --> 00:42:54.947
But I felt like, you know, I was

00:42:54.947 --> 00:42:56.699
gettin' calls from the White House. Like,

00:42:56.699 --> 00:43:00.703
call—like, "Make an Instagram post to the

00:43:00.703 --> 00:43:02.621
kids and tell 'em to go home during the

00:43:02.621 --> 00:43:04.206
Baltimore riots."

00:43:04.206 --> 00:43:05.874
I said, "That's got to be the stupidest

00:43:05.874 --> 00:43:06.750
thing I ever heard."

00:43:07.793 --> 00:43:08.586
Why?

00:43:08.586 --> 00:43:11.255
Because ain't no—nobody who's rioting

00:43:11.255 --> 00:43:12.590
is lookin' at their damn phone.

00:43:12.590 --> 00:43:13.340
They damn sure not out there

00:43:13.340 --> 00:43:14.717
takin' pictures like, "We out here

00:43:14.717 --> 00:43:15.426
gettin' stuff."

00:43:16.719 --> 00:43:17.845
I'm not about to do that.

00:43:17.845 --> 00:43:19.096
- And who couldn't you relate to more,

00:43:19.096 --> 00:43:21.432
the White House or these kids protestin'?
- The kids!

00:43:21.432 --> 00:43:24.018
Cuz I know why it got to that point.

00:43:24.018 --> 00:43:26.812
I work with Booker T. Washington in Baltimore.

00:43:26.812 --> 00:43:28.731
They don't give these kids a chance.

00:43:28.731 --> 00:43:30.733
These kids come down the street,

00:43:30.733 --> 00:43:32.776
and there's more abandoned buildings, just

00:43:32.776 --> 00:43:34.153
like you've seen on "The Wire." They

00:43:34.153 --> 00:43:36.071
walkin' past those abandoned buildings.

00:43:36.071 --> 00:43:38.741
They might have one that's bein' housed.

00:43:38.741 --> 00:43:39.491
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:43:39.491 --> 00:43:43.329
So, these kids go to school with no hope in

00:43:43.329 --> 00:43:44.622
Baltimore, man.

00:43:44.622 --> 00:43:45.289
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:43:45.289 --> 00:43:49.293
So, it was easy for me to deny that because

00:43:49.293 --> 00:43:50.294
they're the root of it.

00:43:51.086 --> 00:43:52.546
The government won't allow you to do

00:43:52.546 --> 00:43:53.339
nothin' in the schools.

00:43:53.339 --> 00:43:54.506
The government won't allow you to take

00:43:54.506 --> 00:43:55.174
care of our kids.

00:43:55.174 --> 00:43:56.091
The kids are our future.

00:43:56.091 --> 00:43:56.634
How,

00:43:56.634 --> 00:43:59.762
how as a human being, as a grown person,

00:43:59.762 --> 00:44:03.307
that you deny a kid opportunity? A kid

00:44:03.307 --> 00:44:04.516
is innocent.

00:44:04.516 --> 00:44:06.685
You got kids raisin' kids in Baltimore,

00:44:06.685 --> 00:44:08.937
man, because they mamas are strung out on

00:44:08.937 --> 00:44:10.105
stuff.
- Yeah.

00:44:10.105 --> 00:44:10.939
- You know what I'm sayin'?

00:44:10.939 --> 00:44:12.191
That's around the world.

00:44:12.191 --> 00:44:14.818
But what I know, in Baltimore, we need help.

00:44:15.569 --> 00:44:17.363
We need help, and and don't get me wrong,

00:44:17.363 --> 00:44:19.114
Baltimore is not a bad city.

00:44:19.114 --> 00:44:20.908
We got a bad rep, but when you go visit

00:44:20.908 --> 00:44:22.159
Baltimore—
- I have a good time, man.

00:44:22.159 --> 00:44:23.077
- You gonna have a great time.

00:44:23.077 --> 00:44:23.702
- I have a good time.

00:44:23.702 --> 00:44:24.745
- You have a great time, man.

00:44:24.745 --> 00:44:27.373
Go visit, go visit Bygone and—well, you can't

00:44:27.373 --> 00:44:28.832
get in the Elk Room unless you know me,

00:44:28.832 --> 00:44:29.416
you're with me.

00:44:30.084 --> 00:44:30.876
- I'm cool witchu.

00:44:30.876 --> 00:44:31.669
- Yeah, you cool, man.

00:44:31.669 --> 00:44:33.087
- I'm ready.
- You know what I'm sayin'?

00:44:33.087 --> 00:44:35.673
But they got a lotta nice spots, and be

00:44:35.673 --> 00:44:37.257
more—you know what I'm sayin'?

00:44:37.257 --> 00:44:38.676
Well, you can go and enjoy yourself

00:44:38.676 --> 00:44:41.387
and have a good time, and a lotta history there.

00:44:41.387 --> 00:44:42.221
you know?

00:44:42.221 --> 00:44:43.013
- I admired—

00:44:43.013 --> 00:44:44.139
I mean, we can go to your Hall of Fame

00:44:44.139 --> 00:44:45.307
speech now as you're wearin' the Hall of Fame

00:44:45.307 --> 00:44:47.351
ring around your neck. I admired how you

00:44:47.351 --> 00:44:48.811
used the platform to speak about Black

00:44:48.811 --> 00:44:50.604
Lives Matter and police brutality.

00:44:51.605 --> 00:44:54.191
You spoke earlier about how you felt the,

00:44:54.191 --> 00:44:56.527
the, the platform that you had and the

00:44:56.527 --> 00:44:58.278
prominence that you have, the weight on

00:44:58.278 --> 00:44:59.530
your shoulders to do these things and

00:44:59.530 --> 00:45:00.698
speak out on these issues.

00:45:00.698 --> 00:45:01.240
- Yeah.

00:45:01.240 --> 00:45:03.283
- But why in that particular space, when

00:45:03.283 --> 00:45:05.119
everyone's celebrating you, that you

00:45:05.119 --> 00:45:07.162
decided to give light on Black Lives Matter

00:45:07.162 --> 00:45:07.830
and police brutality?

00:45:07.830 --> 00:45:09.873
- Cuz that moment wasn't about me.

00:45:10.582 --> 00:45:13.627
I didn't get there by myself, so why am I

00:45:13.627 --> 00:45:15.546
gonna get up there and talk about what

00:45:15.546 --> 00:45:17.464
you already saw on the highlight reel?

00:45:17.965 --> 00:45:18.590
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:45:18.590 --> 00:45:19.925
You already know what Ed did.

00:45:19.925 --> 00:45:21.427
Now you wanna know what,

00:45:21.427 --> 00:45:23.637
what was Ed off the football field?

00:45:23.637 --> 00:45:24.388
What, what—who

00:45:24.388 --> 00:45:26.765
were the people in his life, you know,

00:45:26.765 --> 00:45:28.934
to help him get, get to that point?

00:45:28.934 --> 00:45:31.061
What are the things that affect you as a

00:45:31.061 --> 00:45:32.354
human being?

00:45:32.354 --> 00:45:33.063
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:45:33.063 --> 00:45:34.398
And that's the time to do it.

00:45:34.398 --> 00:45:35.274
You know?

00:45:35.274 --> 00:45:37.109
Nothin' to him, but that's why I thought

00:45:37.109 --> 00:45:39.027
T.O. should've went, because now you have the

00:45:39.027 --> 00:45:42.698
platform to say all the things you wanna say.

00:45:42.698 --> 00:45:43.866
You know?

00:45:43.866 --> 00:45:47.411
So, for me, it was just, it was just tryin'

00:45:47.411 --> 00:45:49.496
to bring light to the world, man.

00:45:49.496 --> 00:45:52.082
Like, all police are not bad, dude.

00:45:52.082 --> 00:45:53.542
My brother's a police officer.

00:45:53.917 --> 00:45:57.129
I mean, he played with me and, um, he's

00:45:57.129 --> 00:46:01.133
actually Miami Beach police, you know, so.

00:46:02.426 --> 00:46:06.180
It's just so much, man, that, that was on my mind.

00:46:06.180 --> 00:46:07.931
There's a lotta stuff I actually left out

00:46:07.931 --> 00:46:09.433
because they was, like, hittin' the time.

00:46:09.433 --> 00:46:10.768
You know, they only give you, like, 10

00:46:10.768 --> 00:46:12.019
minutes or 15 minutes to speak.

00:46:12.019 --> 00:46:13.228
- Before, they used to let everybody speak

00:46:13.228 --> 00:46:14.438
for as long as they wanted to, and
then they started—

00:46:14.438 --> 00:46:15.522
- Exactly.

00:46:15.522 --> 00:46:17.608
You know, but it is, I mean, it gets long.

00:46:17.608 --> 00:46:18.150
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:46:18.192 --> 00:46:19.318
You got a lotta old guys

00:46:19.318 --> 00:46:20.068
there, sittin' there.

00:46:20.068 --> 00:46:21.236
I'm gonnna be there this year and be

00:46:21.236 --> 00:46:23.363
there every year, smokin' a cigar. You

00:46:23.363 --> 00:46:24.782
know, listenin' to my guys.

00:46:24.782 --> 00:46:27.075
But that's the time, man, at which you

00:46:27.075 --> 00:46:28.827
can bring light to the world, man.

00:46:28.827 --> 00:46:30.662
You can, you can help somebody out.

00:46:30.662 --> 00:46:32.748
You know, you can, you can talk about

00:46:32.748 --> 00:46:35.042
issues that affected you and people would

00:46:35.042 --> 00:46:36.668
never know. Like, thinkin' you're perfect.

00:46:36.668 --> 00:46:39.004
People, people put us on a high pedestal because

00:46:39.004 --> 00:46:39.838
we played a sport.

00:46:39.838 --> 00:46:41.799
When in all actual reality, we're

00:46:41.799 --> 00:46:44.218
human just like everybody else.
- That's real.

00:46:44.218 --> 00:46:45.511
- We're no different.

00:46:45.511 --> 00:46:47.805
We just worked our ass off to play, to be

00:46:47.805 --> 00:46:48.597
really good.

00:46:48.597 --> 00:46:50.265
Just watch. You work your ass off, you can

00:46:50.265 --> 00:46:51.266
do the same thing.

00:46:51.767 --> 00:46:52.684
It's no different.

00:46:52.684 --> 00:46:54.978
It really is just, you know, the heart

00:46:54.978 --> 00:46:55.896
gotta be there.

00:46:55.896 --> 00:46:56.605
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:46:56.605 --> 00:46:59.233
So, at the end of that day, we human beings, man.

00:46:59.233 --> 00:47:00.484
And that's what I tell a lotta pictures

00:47:00.484 --> 00:47:02.319
I got on my 'Gram. I'll be like, "No pics."

00:47:02.569 --> 00:47:04.112
You know, I got this "no pic" thing goin'

00:47:04.112 --> 00:47:06.031
around cuz it is outta hand, man!

00:47:06.031 --> 00:47:07.866
There's a camera everywhere, and it's not

00:47:07.866 --> 00:47:09.910
just these cameras. It's the cameras you

00:47:09.910 --> 00:47:11.453
got in your phone, in your pocket.

00:47:11.453 --> 00:47:14.832
You know, everybody got a camera. From a,

00:47:14.832 --> 00:47:20.045
from a seven-, eight-year-old to a 88-year-old,

00:47:20.045 --> 00:47:22.339
you know? And everybody feel like

00:47:22.339 --> 00:47:24.383
they entitled to your time, your picture.

00:47:24.383 --> 00:47:26.635
Come on, man. I'm just as human as you.

00:47:27.177 --> 00:47:28.303
Walkin' down the street.

00:47:29.638 --> 00:47:31.348
"Hey, Ed, what's up man?"

00:47:31.348 --> 00:47:31.682
"Cool."

00:47:32.015 --> 00:47:32.641
"How you doin'?"

00:47:32.641 --> 00:47:34.351
It's like you're seein' your boy down in—

00:47:34.351 --> 00:47:36.895
